WN.lb(29,{"mt_up_Umo86Rn":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Two little pink things look the same. One is a sweet and one is a tablet. What do you do?","choices":["Ask a grown-up first","Eat the pinker one","Eat both"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you always tell by looking?","explain":"You cannot always tell sweets from tablets by looking, so ask a grown-up first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Your sister's medicine helped her cough. You have a cough too. Can you have some?","choices":["No, it is only for her","Yes, it will help me","Yes, just a little"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who was the medicine given to, you or her?","explain":"Medicine is only for the person it was given to. Ask a grown-up about your cough."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Is more medicine better?","choices":["No, too much can make you poorly","Yes, it works faster","Yes, if it tastes nice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who decides how much you take?","explain":"Too much medicine can make you very poorly. Only take the amount a grown-up gives you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A bottle of shampoo has no red diamond. Can you drink it?","choices":["No, it is not a drink","Yes, no sign means safe","Yes, if it smells nice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is shampoo a drink, or is it for washing hair?","explain":"No sign does not mean safe. Shampoo is for hair, not for drinking."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You find {n} washing pods on the table. How many should you touch?","answer":"n*0","hint":"Washing pods can look like sweets. Should you touch them?","explain":"Touch 0 of the {n} pods. Leave them and tell a grown-up.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is food you know?","choices":["An apple from the fruit bowl","A purple drink under the sink","Little pills in a tub"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one did a grown-up give you to eat?","explain":"The apple is food. A drink under the sink and pills in a tub could hurt you, so ask first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Vitamins look like sweets. Can you eat them from the cupboard?","choices":["No, they are medicine too","Yes, they are sweets","Yes, if you only eat one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are vitamins food or medicine?","explain":"Vitamins count as medicine. Only a grown-up gives them to you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Who can give you medicine at the doctor?","choices":["The nurse, with Mum there","A boy in the waiting room","Nobody, take it yourself"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is a grown-up looking after you?","explain":"The nurse, with Mum there, is a trusted grown-up. Never take medicine from another child."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A tub on the shelf has {n} pink tablets that look like sweets. How many can you eat without asking a grown-up?","answer":"n*0","hint":"Tablets can look like sweets. Who do you ask first?","explain":"You can eat 0 of the {n} tablets without asking. They could be medicine, so ask a grown-up first.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Your friend finds a bottle of cleaner. What do you say?","choices":["Leave it, let's tell a grown-up","Let's taste it","Let's carry it to the park"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do we do with something that could hurt us?","explain":"Leave it where it is and tell a grown-up. Do not taste it or carry it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"There are {n} bottles under the sink and none of them are drinks. How many could you drink?","answer":"n*0","hint":"Things under the sink are cleaners, not drinks. How many are drinks?","explain":"None of the {n} bottles are drinks, so you could drink 0 of them. Cleaners can hurt you.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Dad gives you 1 spoon of medicine. The medicine tastes nice and there are {n} spoons left in the bottle. How many more spoons should you take?","answer":"n*0","hint":"Who decides how much medicine you have? Is more better?","explain":"You take 0 more spoons. Only the amount a grown-up gives you, because too much can make you poorly, even with {n} spoons left.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You find tablets on the floor. What do you do?","choices":["Leave them and tell a grown-up","Pick them up for Mum","Taste one to see"],"answer":0,"hint":"Should you touch or taste something you find?","explain":"Leave them where they are and tell a grown-up. Do not touch them or taste them."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Who can give you medicine?","choices":["A grown-up who looks after you","A friend at nursery","You, from the cupboard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about who keeps you safe.","explain":"Only a trusted grown-up who looks after you, like Mum, Dad, Grandma or a nurse, gives you medicine."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A bottle has a red diamond on it. What does that mean?","choices":["It can hurt you","It is a drink","It is a toy"],"answer":0,"hint":"A red diamond is a warning sign.","explain":"A red diamond means the bottle can hurt you. It is for grown-ups only."}],"explain":{"kid":["Medicine can help you feel better when you are poorly. But the wrong medicine, or too much, can make you very ill.","Only a grown-up who looks after you can give you medicine. That could be Mum, Dad, Grandma or a nurse. Your friend's medicine is only for your friend.","Some tablets look like sweets. Some cleaners look like juice. You cannot always tell by looking. A red diamond on a bottle means it can hurt you.","If you find tablets or a bottle, do not touch it and do not taste it. Leave it where it is and tell a grown-up."],"adult":["Ages 4 to 5. The PSHE Association programme of study for Key Stage 1 covers how medicines help people stay healthy and keeping safe with household products, and the EYFS early learning goal for Managing Self includes making healthy choices. Children under 5 are the age group most often admitted to hospital after swallowing medicines or household chemicals. Key words: medicine, tablet, poorly, trusted grown-up, warning sign, ask first.","Children cannot reliably tell sweets from tablets, or juice from coloured cleaner, by looking. Washing capsules look like squishy sweets and are a known danger to young children. Teach the habit rather than the details: if it is not food you were given, ask a grown-up first. Show your child the red-bordered diamond on bottles as a warning sign, but be careful: many unsafe things have no sign at all.","Common mistakes: thinking medicine that helped a friend or sibling will help them too; thinking more medicine works faster; picking up a found tablet or bottle to be helpful; and thinking a bottle with no warning sign is safe to drink. Vitamins and gummies count as medicine. If a child has swallowed something, call NHS 111, or 999 if they are drowsy, struggling to breathe or fitting, and keep the packet.","In the lab your child flips look-alike pairs (sweets and tablets, juice and cleaner, a jelly sweet and a washing pod), chooses who can give medicine in three places, sorts bottles into drinks and not drinks using the red diamond, picks what to do with three found items, and sorts six medicine cards into safe and not safe."]},"familyTip":"Play 'Ask first' at home. Point to things in the kitchen and bathroom and ask: food, or ask a grown-up first? Show your child the red diamond on a cleaning bottle. Keep medicines, vitamins, washing pods and cleaners up high or locked away, and put them back straight after use.","keyIdea":"Medicine can help when you are poorly, but the wrong medicine, or too much, can make you very ill. So only a trusted grown-up who looks after you gives you medicine, and only your own. Tablets can look like sweets and cleaners can look like juice, so never taste something you find. Leave it where it is and tell a grown-up.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Food I know, or ask a grown-up first? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Food or drink I know","Ask a grown-up first"],"items":[["An apple from the fruit bowl",0],["Pink things in a tub",1],["A cup of milk from Mum",0],["A purple drink under the sink",1],["A squishy coloured pod",1],["My tea on my plate",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmeds","caption":"Flagship lab: flip look-alike pairs, pick who gives medicine, sort bottles by the red diamond, choose what to do with a find, sort safe or not.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_xvcHxCkvrp":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You are outside. Your teddy is still inside. What do you do?","choices":["Stay out and tell a grown-up","Run back in for teddy","Open the door to look"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a toy worth going back into a fire for?","explain":"Never go back in. A teddy can be bought again. Stay out and tell a grown-up or a firefighter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You feel scared when the alarm beeps. Where should you go?","choices":["Out of the house","Under my bed","In the cupboard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can firefighters find you under a bed?","explain":"Hiding feels safe but it is not. Smoke gets in, and nobody can find you. Get out of the house."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which number do you call for a fire in the UK?","choices":["999","123","111"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the same number three times.","explain":"999 is the emergency number in the UK. Ask for Fire. 111 is for feeling unwell."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Kitty the cat is still inside. What is safe?","choices":["Tell a firefighter Kitty is inside","Go back in and call her","Leave the meeting place to look"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who has the special kit to go into a fire?","explain":"Firefighters have masks and kit to go in. Tell them Kitty is inside and stay out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Your home has {n} smoke alarms. {k} are upstairs. How many are downstairs?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the {k} upstairs alarms away from all {n}.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} smoke alarms downstairs.","params":{"n":[4,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The operator asks which service you need. There is a fire. What do you say?","choices":["Fire","Police","Ambulance"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who comes with a fire engine?","explain":"Ask for Fire. Firefighters come in a fire engine to put out fires."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} children are at the meeting place. {k} more come out. How many children are there now?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Start at {n} and count on {k} more children.","explain":"{n} and {k} more make {=n+k} children at the meeting place.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"The alarm beeps and you want your shoes. What do you do?","choices":["Leave them and get out","Look for them first","Wait in my room"],"answer":0,"hint":"What matters most: shoes, or getting out?","explain":"Every second counts in a fire. Leave your shoes and things, and get out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why do we get down low in smoke?","choices":["The clean air is near the floor","So the fire cannot see us","It is a game"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which way does smoke go: up or down?","explain":"Smoke rises to the ceiling first, so the cleaner air is down near the floor."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The smoke alarm beeps. What does it mean?","choices":["Get out now","Time to get up","Go and find the fire"],"answer":0,"hint":"A smoke alarm is a warning. What is it warning you about?","explain":"The beep warns that there may be smoke or fire, so it means get out now."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} people in your family. {k} are at the meeting place. How many more are you waiting for?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start with all {n} people and take away the {k} who are already there.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so you wait for {=n-k} more and tell a firefighter if anyone is missing.","params":{"n":[4,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"There is smoke in the room. How do you move?","choices":["Down low, near the floor","Standing up tall","Hiding under the bed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Smoke goes up. Where is the clean air?","explain":"Smoke rises, so the cleaner air is near the floor. Get down low and get out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You crawl {n} steps to the door, then walk {k} steps to the meeting place. How many steps is that?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Put the {n} crawling steps and the {k} walking steps together.","explain":"{n} and {k} more make {=n+k} steps to get to the meeting place.","params":{"n":[3,6],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You are out of the house. Where do you wait?","choices":["At our meeting place","Next to the front door","In the shed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where will your family look for you?","explain":"Go to the meeting place your family picked, away from the house, so everyone knows you are safe."}],"explain":{"kid":["A smoke alarm makes a loud beep. The beep means: get out now. Shout \"Fire!\" so everyone hears.","Do not hide under the bed or in a cupboard. Smoke can get in there, and the firefighters will not find you.","Smoke goes up high. The clean air is low, near the floor. Get down low and go. Leave your toys.","Go to your meeting place outside and stay there. Never go back in, not for a toy or a pet. Tell a firefighter. A grown-up calls 999."],"adult":["Ages 4 to 5. The PSHE Association Programme of Study (2020) asks KS1 pupils to learn how to keep safe at home and what to do in an emergency, including how to call 999. UK fire and rescue services give families the same short routine: get out, stay out, call 999. Key words: smoke alarm, smoke, get out, meeting place, firefighter, 999.","Smoke is the main danger in a house fire, more than flames. Hot smoke rises and fills a room from the ceiling down, so the air near the floor stays cleaner and cooler for longer. That is why children are taught to get down low. Toys and pets are left behind because seconds matter, and firefighters, not family members, go back in.","Common mistakes: hiding under a bed or in a wardrobe because they feel scared; stopping to find a toy or a pet; standing tall in smoke; waiting right next to the house; going back in; and not knowing the number. Be careful not to make the alarm frightening. Let your child press the test button and practise the plan as a calm game.","In the lab your child taps a smoke alarm and picks the first move, drags a child along the floor under the smoke and past a teddy to the door, takes the family to the meeting-place tree, picks what to do when Kitty the cat is still inside, and dials 9, 9, 9, asks for Fire and gives an address. In the USA and Canada the number is 911, and 112 works across Europe."]},"familyTip":"Agree a meeting place outside, like a tree or a neighbour's gate, and walk there together. Press the test button on your smoke alarm with your child so the beep is not scary, and practise saying your address and the number 999.","keyIdea":"If there is fire or smoke, or the smoke alarm beeps, get out and stay out. Shout \"Fire!\", never hide under a bed or in a cupboard, get down low under smoke, and leave toys and pets. Go to your family's meeting place, never go back inside, and call 999 once you are out.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Safe in a fire, or not safe? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Safe in a fire","Not safe"],"items":[["Get out when the alarm beeps",0],["Hide in a cupboard",1],["Crawl low under smoke",0],["Go back in for teddy",1],["Wait at the meeting place",0],["Stop to find your shoes and toys",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggetout","caption":"Flagship lab: hear the alarm, crawl under smoke past the toys, go to the meeting place, stay out for the cat, and dial 999.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_2le11aazim":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Sam says, \"I can't eat nuts.\" What do you say?","choices":["OK! Want to play?","Just try a tiny bit","I'll pick the nuts out"],"answer":0,"hint":"When a friend says no to food, what should we do?","explain":"Say OK and stop asking. Even a tiny bit, or a cake with nuts picked out, can make Sam very ill."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"After lunch, Leo has puffy lips and itchy bumps. What do you do?","choices":["Tell a grown-up straight away","Wait and see","Keep it quiet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Puffy lips and itchy bumps are signs. Who can help fast?","explain":"Tell a grown-up straight away. Allergy signs can get worse quickly, and grown-ups know what to do."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Leo can't have milk. Can he eat cheese?","choices":["No, cheese is made from milk","Yes, cheese is not milk","Yes, a little is fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is cheese made from?","explain":"Cheese is made from milk, so it can make Leo ill. Milk hides in lots of foods."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"You have {n} grapes. Your friend asks for {k}. You keep your lunch and share a game instead. How many grapes do you still have?","answer":"n","hint":"Did you give any grapes away?","explain":"You still have {n} grapes. We don't share food, but we can share a game.","params":{"n":[3,8],"k":[1,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which one is a sign to get a grown-up?","choices":["A cough and hard to breathe","A big yawn","Laughing at a joke"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one shows a body is having trouble?","explain":"Coughing and trouble breathing can mean a bad allergy. Get a grown-up straight away."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia says she can't eat egg. Is she just being fussy?","choices":["No, egg can make her ill","Yes, she is fussy","Yes, she just doesn't like it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is an allergy about liking a food, or about the body?","explain":"An allergy is not being fussy. Egg can make Mia's body very ill, so we say OK."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} friends at your table each eat their own lunch. How many lunch boxes are on the table?","answer":"n","hint":"Each friend has one lunch box. Count the friends.","explain":"{n} friends, one lunch box each, makes {n} lunch boxes. Everyone eats their own.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A friend says, \"Go on, have my cake. Nobody will know.\" What do you do?","choices":["Say no thanks, and tell a grown-up if unsure","Eat it quickly","Hide it for later"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a food rule OK to break if nobody sees?","explain":"Food rules keep us safe even when nobody is looking. Say no thanks, and ask a grown-up if you are not sure."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What can we share at lunch time?","choices":["A game or a chat","Our sandwiches","A bite of cake"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can we share that is not food?","explain":"We share games, chats and fun, and keep our food to ourselves."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Peanuts are fine for you. Are they fine for everyone?","choices":["No, some people are allergic","Yes, food is food","Yes, if it is a tiny bit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Sam. What happens in his body?","explain":"Every body is different. A food that is fine for you can make someone with an allergy very ill."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} children are at your lunch table. 1 child has a nut allergy. How many children have no nut allergy?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Count everyone at the table, then take away the 1 with the allergy.","explain":"{n} children, take away 1, leaves {=n-1} with no nut allergy. We still don't share food with any of them.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Your friend has no allergy badge. Can you share your crisps?","choices":["No, we eat our own lunch","Yes, no badge means safe","Yes, if we swap"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you always tell who has an allergy just by looking?","explain":"No. Some allergies are not on a badge, so we eat our own lunch and share games instead."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You see {n} tiny bits of peanut on your hands. You wash off 2. How many are left to wash off?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Start with all the bits, then take away the 2 you washed off.","explain":"{n} bits, take away 2, leaves {=n-2}. Keep scrubbing with soap and water until there are none.","params":{"n":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You ate a peanut bar. How do you clean your hands?","choices":["Soap and water","Hand gel","Wipe them on my trousers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one washes the food bits away, not just the germs?","explain":"Soap and water washes food bits off. Gel kills germs but leaves the peanut on your hands."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every body is different. Peanuts are fine for Ava. But Sam is allergic to peanuts. His body gets very ill, even from a tiny bit. So we eat our own lunch. We do not share or swap food. We share games and fun instead.","Tiny bits of food stay on hands. Wash them with soap and water. Hand gel does not take food bits off.","If a friend says \"I can't eat that\", say OK and stop asking. Do not say \"just try a bit\".","Does a friend get itchy bumps, puffy lips, a cough or a bad tummy after eating? Tell a grown-up straight away."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. In England the statutory Health Education guidance (DfE, 2019) asks primary pupils to learn the facts and science relating to allergies, and how to get help in an emergency. A food allergy is the immune system reacting to a food protein as if it were harmful. The most common triggers in children are milk, egg, peanuts, tree nuts, sesame, fish and shellfish. UK labels must show 14 major allergens.","The NHS lists the signs: an itchy mouth, a raised itchy red rash (hives), swelling of the face, lips, tongue or around the eyes, tummy pain and being sick. A severe reaction (anaphylaxis) can cause trouble breathing, a wheeze or cough, or feeling faint, and can happen within minutes. It is treated with an adrenaline auto-injector and a 999 call. A child's job is simple: tell a grown-up straight away and do not wait.","Common mistakes: thinking a food that is safe for me is safe for everyone; thinking no badge or no visible sign means it is fine to share; believing a tiny bit, or a food with the nuts picked out, is safe; not knowing that milk hides in cheese and butter; cleaning hands with gel (Perry and colleagues, 2004, found soap and water or wipes removed peanut protein, but hand gel often did not); keeping quiet; and calling an allergy fussy eating. Be careful not to frighten: the rules are what keep friends safe.","In the lab your child drags a looking glass over Ava, Sam and Leo to see that peanuts are fine for two of them but not Sam, and that Leo has a milk allergy instead. They sort six lunch cards into Do and Don't, and scrub peanut bits off hands, finding that gel and trousers do not work. They reply kindly when three friends say no to food, then spot four signs on Sam and choose to tell a grown-up."]},"familyTip":"Practise the words together: \"No thanks, I can't eat that\" and \"OK! Want to play?\" If your child has an allergy, help them say what it is and who to tell. Wash hands with soap and water before and after eating, and remind them that sharing at lunch means sharing games, not food.","keyIdea":"Some people are allergic to a food, such as peanuts, milk or egg. A food that is fine for you can make their body very ill, even from a tiny bit. So we eat our own lunch and do not share or swap food. Wash food off your hands with soap and water. When a friend says \"I can't eat that\", say OK and stop asking. If a friend gets itchy bumps, puffy lips, a cough or a bad tummy after eating, tell a grown-up straight away.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Safe with food allergies, or not safe? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Safe","Not safe"],"items":[["Eat your own lunch",0],["Swap a sandwich with a friend",1],["Wash hands with soap and water",0],["Pick the nuts out and share it",1],["Tell a grown-up if a friend feels ill",0],["Say \"just try a tiny bit\"",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagallergy","caption":"Flagship lab: see one food act differently in three friends, sort lunch rules, scrub peanut bits off, reply kindly and spot signs to get help.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_mD1_BxIPz-":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Who are doctors, nurses and dentists?","choices":["Grown-ups whose job is to help us stay healthy","People who tell you off","People to keep secrets from"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what their job is for.","explain":"Their job is to help us stay healthy and to help when we are ill."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Who stays with you at the visit?","choices":["My grown-up","Nobody","A stranger from the waiting room"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who brings you to the visit?","explain":"Your parent or carer stays with you, and the doctor tells you what they will do."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You feel worried before the visit. What helps?","choices":["Ask what will happen","Run and hide","Keep the worry a secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"What makes a visit less of a surprise?","explain":"Asking what will happen helps. Hiding or keeping the worry secret makes it feel bigger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The doctor uses a little light. What does it look at?","choices":["Inside your ears","Your feet","Your hair"],"answer":0,"hint":"It has a thin cone on the end.","explain":"The ear light (an otoscope) shines inside your ear so the doctor can see. It does not hurt."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You take {n} slow breaths, then {k} more. How many slow breaths is that?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Count all the breaths together.","explain":"{n} and {k} more make {=n+k} slow breaths. Slow breaths help a worry go down.","params":{"n":[1,3],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Where does the thermometer go for a check?","choices":["Under your arm","On your teeth","On your knee"],"answer":0,"hint":"For young children, it goes in the armpit.","explain":"For children under 5 the thermometer goes under the arm. It checks how hot you are."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How many milk teeth does a child have?","choices":["20","32","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Grown-ups have more teeth than children.","explain":"A child has 20 milk teeth: 10 on top and 10 on the bottom. Grown-ups have up to 32."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The dentist checked {n} top teeth and {k} bottom teeth. How many teeth is that?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Add the top teeth and the bottom teeth.","explain":"{n} top and {k} bottom teeth make {=n+k} teeth checked.","params":{"n":[2,10],"k":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Is going to the dentist a punishment?","choices":["No, it keeps us healthy","Yes, if you were naughty","Yes, if your teeth are messy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does everyone go, even children who were good?","explain":"Everyone goes to stay healthy. A visit is never a punishment."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"What happens first at a visit?","choices":["Say my name at the desk","Have the check","Go home"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you do as soon as you arrive?","explain":"First you say your name at the desk. Then you wait, go in with your grown-up, and have the check."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why do we go to the dentist when nothing hurts?","choices":["For a check-up, to keep teeth healthy","Because we were naughty","We never go if nothing hurts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can the dentist spot a problem before it hurts?","explain":"A check-up lets the dentist spot a problem early, before it hurts. It is never a punishment."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The dentist has counted {n} of your teeth. They count 1 more. How many teeth now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 1 more tooth.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. A child has 20 milk teeth when they are all through.","params":{"n":[4,19]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A tooth feels sore in the check. What do you do?","choices":["Put my hand up and say it hurts","Keep quiet and be brave","Shut my mouth tight"],"answer":0,"hint":"How will the dentist know it hurts?","explain":"Put your hand up and tell them. Then the dentist knows which tooth to help."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You wait in the waiting room. {n} children go in before you. Then it is your turn. How many go in, with you?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count the children before you, then add you.","explain":"{n} children and you make {=n+1}. Waiting your turn is part of the visit.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What does a stethoscope do?","choices":["Listens to your heart and breathing","Looks inside your ears","Cleans your teeth"],"answer":0,"hint":"It has ear pieces for the doctor, and a round end for your chest.","explain":"A stethoscope listens to your heart and breathing. It may feel cold, but it does not hurt."}],"explain":{"kid":["A doctor, a nurse and a dentist help you stay well. You go when you feel ill. You go for a check when you feel fine too.","Your grown-up stays with you. The doctor tells you what they will do. You can ask: what will you do next?","The tools just look and listen. A stethoscope hears your heart. A little mirror helps the dentist see your teeth.","If it hurts, put your hand up and say so. That helps them help you. It is OK to feel a bit scared. Hold a hand and take slow breaths."],"adult":["Ages 4 to 5. Development Matters (2021) asks Reception children to know and talk about what supports their health and wellbeing, including toothbrushing. The government's health education guidance for primary schools includes dental health and regular check-ups at the dentist. The NHS advises taking children to the dentist as soon as milk teeth appear, and NICE guidance sets check-ups every 3 to 12 months for under-18s, as the dentist advises.","Knowing what will happen lowers worry. A typical visit is: check in, wait, go in with a parent or carer, then the check. A stethoscope listens to the heart and breathing, an otoscope (ear light) looks inside the ear, and for children under 5 the NHS advises a digital thermometer under the armpit. A child has 20 milk teeth, and the dentist counts and checks them with a small mirror.","Common mistakes: thinking a visit is a punishment (often because the dentist or an injection was used as a threat); thinking you only go when something hurts; and staying quiet about pain to be brave. Be careful with body safety too: a parent or carer stays in the room, and a doctor or nurse should say what they will do and ask first. Teach a hand-up signal for pain or a rest.","In the lab your child sorts real reasons for a visit from worries, puts the parts of a visit in order, and drags each tool to the place it checks. Then they keep still while a dentist mirror moves tooth by tooth and put a hand up when one feels sore. Last, they bring a worry meter down by tapping things that help."]},"familyTip":"Before a visit, play doctors or dentists at home: count teeth with a spoon as the mirror, listen to a teddy's chest. Say what will happen in order. Agree a signal, such as a raised hand, for \"that hurts\" or \"I need a rest\". Never use the dentist or a jab as a threat, as it teaches that a visit is a punishment.","keyIdea":"Doctors, nurses and dentists are grown-ups whose job is to help us stay healthy. We go when we feel poorly, and we also go for check-ups when nothing hurts. A visit is never a punishment. Your grown-up stays with you, and you can ask what will happen. The tools only look or listen. If something hurts, put your hand up and tell them.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help at a doctor or dentist visit? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["This helps","This does not help"],"items":[["Ask what will happen",0],["Keep quiet when it hurts",1],["Hold my grown-up's hand",0],["Run and hide",1],["Open wide for the mirror",0],["Keep a worry secret",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdocvisit","caption":"Flagship lab: sort why we go, put a visit in order, drag tools to what they check, tell the dentist when it hurts, and calm a worry.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_2mKEQd1pOs":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The floor by the pool is wet. How do you get to the steps?","choices":["Walk","Run","Jump in at the deep end"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens if you run on a wet floor?","explain":"Walk. Running on a wet floor makes you slip. Go in at the shallow end, by the steps."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A red flag is flying at the beach. What does it mean?","choices":["Danger: do not swim there","It is safe to swim","Swim close to the rocks"],"answer":0,"hint":"A red flag is a warning. What should you do?","explain":"A red flag means danger. Never swim there. Swim only between the red and yellow flags."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"There is a ring float, and a friend is in trouble. What do you do?","choices":["Throw it to them","Swim out with it","Keep it on the side"],"answer":0,"hint":"The ring helps only if your friend can hold it.","explain":"Throw the ring to them so they can hold it and float. Do not swim out or jump in."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You fall in. What helps you most?","choices":["Lean back and float like a starfish","Kick and splash hard","Curl up tight"],"answer":0,"hint":"What shape keeps you on the surface without tiring you?","explain":"Lean back and spread your arms and legs like a starfish. Then shout and wave for help."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your grown-up went to get ice cream. Can you go in now?","choices":["No, wait until they are back","Yes, they will be quick","Yes, if I stay in the shallow end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is anyone watching you right now?","explain":"Wait. Your grown-up is not watching now, so you stay out until they are back."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You shout for help {n} times. Then you shout 2 more times, and a grown-up comes. How many shouts is that?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more shouts.","explain":"{n} shouts and 2 more make {=n+2} shouts. Keep shouting until a grown-up comes.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The lake looks calm. Nobody is with you. Is it OK to go in?","choices":["No, wait for a grown-up","Yes, calm water is safe","Yes, if I can touch the bottom"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can water still be cold or deep when it looks calm?","explain":"Calm water can still be cold and deep. Never go in with nobody there."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Where should you stay when you swim at the beach?","choices":["Where your grown-up can see you","Behind the rocks","Far out in deep water"],"answer":0,"hint":"If you need help, can your grown-up reach you quickly?","explain":"Stay where your grown-up can see you, between the red and yellow flags. Behind the rocks or far out, help is too far away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"You throw a ring float to a friend {n} times. Then you throw it 1 more time and they catch it. How many throws is that?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 1 more throw.","explain":"{n} throws and 1 more make {=n+1} throws. The last one reached them.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"You have just walked to the steps at the shallow end. How do you go in?","choices":["Go in by the steps","Jump in at the deep end","Run and jump"],"answer":0,"hint":"You do not know how deep the other end is.","explain":"Go in by the steps, at the shallow end, where you can stand."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You can swim a whole length. Can you go in with no grown-up there?","choices":["No, wait for a grown-up","Yes, I swim well","Yes, if a friend comes too"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does being a strong swimmer change the rule about a grown-up?","explain":"Even strong swimmers only go in with a trusted grown-up watching. A friend is not a grown-up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You walk {n} steps by the pool, then {k} more to the steps. How many steps is that?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Count the first steps, then count on the rest. Keep walking, do not run.","explain":"{n} steps and {k} more make {=n+k} steps. Walking keeps you from slipping.","params":{"n":[2,6],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Where do lifeguards watch at the beach?","choices":["Between the red and yellow flags","By the red flag","Behind the rocks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for two flags, one red and one yellow, close together.","explain":"Lifeguards watch the water between the red and yellow flags. A red flag means danger: do not swim there."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} children are in the water. Then {k} more go in with a grown-up. How many children are in the water?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Count the first group, then count on the children who just went in.","explain":"{n} children and {k} more make {=n+k} children in the water, all with a grown-up watching.","params":{"n":[1,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A friend is in trouble in the water. What do you do first?","choices":["Shout for a grown-up","Jump in after them","Run home and hide"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who can pull them out safely? Call that person first.","explain":"Shout for a grown-up first, so help is coming. Then throw something that floats. Never jump in."}],"explain":{"kid":["Only go in the water if a trusted grown-up is with you and watching. A friend is not a grown-up. Even if you swim well, you still wait.","At the beach, swim between the red and yellow flags. That is where the lifeguard is watching. Stay close, so your grown-up can see you. A red flag means danger: do not swim there.","By a pool, the floor is wet. Walk, do not run, or you will slip. Go in at the shallow end, by the steps.","If a friend is in trouble in the water, shout for a grown-up. Throw a ring float to them. Do not jump in: they could pull you under too. If you fall in, lean back and float like a starfish. Then shout and wave."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. The PSHE Association Programme of Study for key stage 1 asks children to recognise risk in everyday situations, to know ways to keep safe at a beach or swimming pool, and how to get help in an emergency (H29, H32, H36). This lesson applies that to water: pools, beaches, lakes and rivers. The rules come from the RLSS UK and RNLI Water Safety Code (Stop and Think, Stay Together, Float, Call 999 or 112), the RNLI Float to Live advice, and RNLI beach flag meanings.","Children often think being a strong swimmer is enough, or that a friend or a calm-looking lake makes it safe. Water can be cold, deep or moving, and even a short swim can tire a child quickly. At the beach, the red and yellow flags mark the area a lifeguard is watching; a red flag means danger and no swimming. Behind rocks or far out, a grown-up on the sand cannot see or reach them in time.","Common mistakes: going in alone because they swim well; treating a friend as a grown-up; swimming outside the flags or out of sight; running on a wet poolside; jumping in at the deep end; jumping in to rescue someone (they can be pulled under); and kicking hard after falling in, which tires them and can make them breathe in water. The right first help is to shout, throw something that floats, and not go in.","Before a trip, practise the four rules. At the beach, walk to the flags together and say why you swim there. If you step away, even for ice cream, take them with you. In the lab your child decides when it is OK to go in, picks safe places between the flags, walks a wet poolside, helps Sam by shouting and throwing a ring, and builds a starfish float."]},"familyTip":"Before a trip to a pool or the beach, practise the four rules with your child: who must be there, where to swim, how to walk on a wet floor, and what to do if someone is in trouble. At the beach, walk to the red and yellow flags together and say, 'This is where we swim, because the lifeguard is watching here.' If you step away, even for a minute, take them with you.","keyIdea":"Only go in water with a trusted grown-up who is watching you, even if you swim well. At the beach, swim between the red and yellow flags, where a lifeguard is watching, and stay where your grown-up can see you. Walk, do not run, by a pool, and go in at the shallow end. If someone is in trouble in the water, shout for a grown-up and throw something that floats: never jump in after them. If you fall in, lean back and float like a starfish, then shout and wave.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Safe or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Safe to go in","Wait or stay out"],"items":[["Dad is watching you",0],["No grown-up is there",1],["Between the red and yellow flags",0],["A red flag is flying",1],["Walk to the steps",0],["Jump in after a friend",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwatersafe","caption":"Flagship lab: decide when it is OK to go in, pick a safe place on the beach, walk a wet poolside, help someone without jumping in, and float like a starfish.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qDk3tEIni7":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A grown-up says, “Don’t tell your mum. It’s our special secret.” What do you do?","choices":["Tell a trusted grown-up","Keep it: it is special","Keep it, but feel bad"],"answer":0,"hint":"Should a grown-up ask you to hide things from Mum?","explain":"Tell. Calling it special does not make it good. A grown-up should not ask you to keep secrets from Mum."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Someone gives you sweets and says, “Keep quiet.” Is it a good secret now?","choices":["No, it is a worry secret","Yes, I got sweets","Yes, if I like the sweets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Why would someone pay you to keep quiet?","explain":"No. Sweets or presents to keep quiet are a warning sign. Tell a trusted grown-up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You promised to keep a secret. Now it makes you scared. Can you tell?","choices":["Yes, tell a trusted grown-up","No, a promise is a promise","Only if they say I can"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which matters more: the promise or being safe?","explain":"Yes. You can break a promise to keep a worry secret. Telling is the right thing to do."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Who should you tell about a worry secret?","choices":["My teacher or Mum","My teddy","The one who told me to keep it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who can do something to help you?","explain":"Tell a trusted grown-up, like your teacher or Mum. A teddy cannot help, and the person who asked will not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your class plans a party for your teacher on Friday. What is it?","choices":["A good surprise","A worry secret","A secret to keep for ever"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it have a day when everyone finds out?","explain":"A good surprise. Your teacher finds out on Friday, and it makes them happy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"It is Monday. Gran’s party is in {n} days. Then she will know. How many days until Gran finds out?","answer":"n","hint":"The party day is when Gran finds out.","explain":"{n} days. A surprise has a day when everyone finds out.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You tell your teacher a worry secret, but she is busy. What next?","choices":["Tell another trusted grown-up","Stop telling","Tell my teddy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep going until a grown-up listens.","explain":"Tell another trusted grown-up, like Mum, Dad or Gran. Keep telling until someone listens."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which one is a warning sign?","choices":["“Nobody will believe you.”","“Shh! It’s a surprise for Mum.”","“Don’t peek at your present!”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which words try to stop you telling?","explain":"“Nobody will believe you” tries to stop you telling. It is a warning sign. Grown-ups will listen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"{n} friends plan a surprise for Dad. {k} more friends join in. How many friends are in on the surprise?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Start at {n} and count on {k}.","explain":"{n} friends and {k} more make {=n+k} friends. Dad will find out on his birthday.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Will you be in trouble for telling a worry secret?","choices":["No, telling is right","Yes, I will be told off","Yes, if I promised"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do trusted grown-ups want you to do?","explain":"No. You will not be in trouble for telling. Trusted grown-ups want to know so they can help."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You and Dad hide a present for Mum. You will give it to her on Sunday. What is it?","choices":["A good surprise","A worry secret","A secret to keep for ever"],"answer":0,"hint":"Will Mum find out soon? Will she be happy?","explain":"It is a good surprise. Mum finds out on Sunday, and it makes her happy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You keep a surprise for {n} days. Then you wait {k} more days, and everyone finds out. How many days is that?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Start at {n} and count on {k} more days.","explain":"{n} days and {k} more make {=n+k} days. A surprise has an end: then everyone finds out.","params":{"n":[1,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Someone says, “If you tell, you will be in trouble.” What is that?","choices":["A warning sign: tell a grown-up","A good surprise","A reason to keep quiet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would a happy surprise need a scary rule like that?","explain":"It is a warning sign. Tell a trusted grown-up. You will not be in trouble for telling."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You name {n} grown-ups you trust at home and {k} at school. How many grown-ups can you tell?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Count the grown-ups at home, then count on the ones at school.","explain":"{n} and {k} more make {=n+k} trusted grown-ups. If one is busy, you can tell another.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A secret makes your tummy feel sick. What does that tell you?","choices":["It is a worry secret","I am just excited","I must hide it better"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do happy surprises make you feel sick?","explain":"A sick, scared or sad feeling means it is a worry secret. Tell a trusted grown-up."}],"explain":{"kid":["A good surprise is a happy secret. You hide a present or plan a party. Soon everyone finds out, and they smile.","A worry secret is not like that. It makes you feel scared, sad or sick inside. Or someone says you must never tell. Ask: will everyone find out soon? Does it feel happy? If not, it is a worry secret.","Some words are a warning. “It’s our special secret.” “You will be in trouble if you tell.” “Nobody will believe you.” Sweets or a toy to keep quiet are a warning too.","Tell a worry secret to a grown-up you trust, like Mum, Dad, Gran or your teacher. Tell even if you promised. You will not be in trouble. If they are busy, tell someone else."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. The PSHE Association Programme of Study for key stage 1 asks children to learn the difference between good surprises and secrets that should be shared, to recognise when something feels unsafe, and how to ask a trusted adult for help. The DfE Relationships Education guidance (2019) includes knowing when it is right to break a confidence and how to report a concern. The NSPCC PANTS rule “Speak up, someone can help” and its advice to talk about surprises rather than secrets are the model here.","Two simple tests do most of the work at this age. Time: a surprise has an end, a day when everyone finds out. Feeling: a surprise feels exciting; a worry secret feels scary, sad, confusing or sick. Children are also told to notice what the other person says. Safeguarding guidance lists “special secret”, threats (“you’ll get in trouble”, “I’ll hurt you”), “nobody will believe you” and gifts for silence as common grooming and bullying tactics.","Common mistakes: thinking a secret called special or fun must be a good one; thinking a present makes it all right; believing a promise must always be kept; telling only a toy, a friend or the person who asked for the secret; and stopping after one busy adult. Some children also mix up excited butterflies with real worry, so naming body feelings helps.","Use the word surprise at home for happy things, and say you don’t keep secrets in your family. Practise who they would tell and what they would say: “I have a worry secret.” In the lab your child sorts cards, puts each secret on the day it is told, taps worry feelings and warning words, and helps Mia tell a trusted grown-up even though she promised."]},"familyTip":"Use the word surprise for happy things at home: “This is a surprise for Gran. We will tell her on Saturday.” Then say plainly: “In our family we don’t keep secrets, only surprises. If anyone ever asks you to keep a secret that worries you, tell me or your teacher, even if you promised.” Ask your child to name three grown-ups they would tell.","keyIdea":"A good surprise is a happy secret, like a present or a party, and everyone finds out soon. A worry secret makes you feel scared, sad or sick, or someone says you must never tell. Warning signs are words like “It’s our special secret”, “You will be in trouble” or “Nobody will believe you”, and sweets or presents to keep quiet. Always tell a trusted grown-up about a worry secret, even if you promised not to. You will not be in trouble for telling.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Good surprise or worry secret? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Good surprise","Worry secret"],"items":[["A present for Dad on Friday",0],["“Tell, and I will hit you”",1],["A party for Gran on Saturday",0],["“It’s our special secret”",1],["A cake for Mum on Sunday",0],["Sweets to keep quiet",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsecret","caption":"Flagship lab: sort surprises from worry secrets, find the day each is told, spot worry feelings and warning words, and help Mia tell.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_MJNtGaVyKv":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Mum is in the garden. Your little brother fell and his leg looks wrong. What first?","choices":["Run and get Mum","Call 999 without telling Mum","Wait and see"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a grown-up close by?","explain":"Run and get Mum. A grown-up is near, and she will decide if 999 is needed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You are alone with Gran. She fell and will not wake up. What do you do?","choices":["Call 999 yourself","Wait for Mum to get home","Go out to play"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a grown-up help right now?","explain":"Call 999 yourself. Not waking up is an emergency, and no grown-up is there to help."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Someone is breaking into the house next door. Which service do you ask for?","choices":["Police","Fire","Ambulance"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which service deals with danger from a person?","explain":"Police. Fire is for fire and smoke; ambulance is for someone badly hurt or ill."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The phone is locked and you do not know the code. Can you call 999?","choices":["Yes, tap Emergency on the lock screen","No, you need the code","No, you need credit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the bottom of the lock screen.","explain":"Yes. Tap Emergency and dial 999. No code or credit is needed, and the call is free."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your friend says, “Let’s call 999 for fun.” What do you say?","choices":["No, it is only for real emergencies","Yes, if we hang up fast","Yes, it is free"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could happen to someone who really needs help?","explain":"No. A joke call can stop a real emergency getting through."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You practise saying your address with a grown-up {n} times a day for {k} days. How many practices is that?","answer":"n*k","hint":"{n} practices each day, for {k} days. Multiply.","explain":"{n} times {k} is {=n*k}. Knowing your address by heart helps the call handler find you fast.","params":{"n":[1,3],"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You are at the park, not at home. The call handler asks where you are. What helps?","choices":["Say a street sign or big building you can see","Say “I don’t know” and stop","Hang up and walk home"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can you see around you?","explain":"Say what you can see: a street sign, a shop or a big building. It helps them find you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which of these is not an emergency?","choices":["A small paper cut","A kitchen fire","A boy choking who cannot breathe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one can a plaster fix?","explain":"A small paper cut. Tell a grown-up and use a plaster. The others need help now."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"An ambulance is {n} minutes away. The call handler says, “Stay on the line.” After {k} minutes, how many minutes until it comes?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start at {n} minutes and take away the {k} that have passed.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k} minutes. Keep listening to the call handler until help comes.","params":{"n":[8,12],"k":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is an emergency where you need help now?","choices":["Grandad cannot breathe properly","My pencil broke","I cannot find my shoe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is someone badly hurt, not breathing, in a fire or in danger?","explain":"Grandad cannot breathe properly. That needs help now: a grown-up, or 999 if no grown-up can help."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"On a pretend call with a toy phone you say 3 things: where you are, what happened and your name. You practise {n} times. How many things do you say in all?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each practice is 3 things. Count in threes {n} times.","explain":"{n} lots of 3 is {=3*n}. Practising with a toy phone, never a real one, makes a real call easier.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"There is smoke in your house. What do you do first?","choices":["Get out, then call 999","Stay inside and call 999","Hide under the bed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is it safe to make the call?","explain":"Get out first, stay out, then call 999 from outside. Never stay in smoke to call."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The call handler asks you {n} questions. You have answered {k}. How many are left?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start with {n} questions and take away the {k} you answered.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}. Keep answering, and stay on until they say you can go.","params":{"n":[4,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You have told the call handler what happened. What now?","choices":["Stay on the line until they say you can go","Hang up straight away","Call again to check"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who decides when the call is over?","explain":"Stay on the line. The call handler may have more questions or tell you how to help."}],"explain":{"kid":["An emergency is when someone is badly hurt, cannot breathe or will not wake up. A fire is an emergency. So is a person who is in danger. A grazed knee or a lost toy is not.","If a grown-up is near, run and get them. They will know what to do. If no grown-up can help, you can call 999 yourself. In a fire, get out first. Then call from outside.","You do not need the code to call. Tap Emergency on the lock screen and dial 9 9 9. The call is free. In the UK, 112 works too. Other countries use other numbers, like 911.","A call handler will ask which service you need: ambulance, fire or police. Then say where you are, what has happened and your name. Speak slowly. Do not hang up until they say you can go. Never call 999 as a joke or to test it."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 10. The DfE Health Education guidance (2019) says that by the end of primary school pupils should know how to make a clear and efficient call to the emergency services if necessary. The PSHE Association Programme of Study asks key stage 2 pupils to learn when and how to contact the emergency services. In the UK, 999 and 112 both reach the police, fire service, ambulance and coastguard; calls are free and can be made from a locked phone with no credit.","The lab follows the order a child faces. First decide if it is an emergency at all (NHS guidance lists not breathing, not waking up, heavy bleeding and choking among reasons to call). Then decide who acts first: fetching a nearby adult is usually best, calling yourself is right when no adult can help, and in a fire the fire service rule is get out, stay out, call 999. Then dial from the lock screen, name the service, and answer the call handler's questions.","Common mistakes: calling 999 for a small cut or a lost item; making a test or joke call, which ties up a line and can be an offence; staying in a smoky building to phone; thinking a locked phone or no credit means you cannot call; not knowing your address; talking too fast; and hanging up as soon as the problem has been said. Be careful not to make children afraid to call: if they are truly unsure and no adult is there, calling and explaining is the right choice.","Ambulance call handlers usually ask for the address first, then what has happened. When out, children can give a street sign, a shop or a landmark. This lesson covers the call itself; the lesson Fire safety: get out and stay out covers escaping a fire, and Telling a grown-up when something feels wrong covers everyday worries."]},"familyTip":"Practise a pretend call with a toy phone, never a real one: you be the call handler. Ask “Where are you?” and check your child can say your full address, or name a street sign or shop when you are out. Show them where the Emergency button is on your locked phone. If you live outside the UK, teach your own number (for example 112, 911 or 000).","keyIdea":"An emergency is when someone is badly hurt, cannot breathe, will not wake up, there is a fire, or someone is in danger. If a grown-up is near, fetch them fast. If not, call 999 yourself (in a fire, get out first). Say where you are, what has happened and your name, speak slowly, and stay on the line until the call handler says you can go. 999 is never for practice or small bumps.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Help now, or can it wait? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Help now: 999 or a grown-up","Not an emergency"],"items":[["Grandad cannot breathe properly",0],["I grazed my knee",1],["Smoke is coming from the kitchen",0],["My tablet is lost",1],["Someone is breaking in",0],["I want to see if 999 works",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagemcall","caption":"Flagship lab: sort emergencies, pick who to go to first, dial 999 on a locked phone, choose the service, then answer the call handler.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_KoixyMptiW":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You are behind a big parked van. Can the car driver see you?","choices":["No, the van hides me","Yes, I can see the road","Yes, if I wave"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what is between you and the driver.","explain":"The van is in the way, so the driver cannot see a small child behind it. Step out to a clear place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"While you walk across, what should you do?","choices":["Keep looking and listening","Look at your feet","Run to the other side"],"answer":0,"hint":"A car could still come while you are walking.","explain":"Keep looking and listening all the way across, and walk, do not run."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A car is far away but going very fast. Is it safe to cross?","choices":["Maybe not: a fast car comes quickly","Yes, far away is always safe","Yes, if you run"],"answer":0,"hint":"Far away does not always mean lots of time.","explain":"A far, fast car can reach you sooner than you think. Speed is hard to judge, so wait or hold a grown-up's hand."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"The Green Cross Code has 5 steps. You have done {a} of them. How many steps are still to do?","answer":"5-a","hint":"There are 5 steps in all. Take away the ones already done.","explain":"5 steps minus {a} already done leaves {=5-a} still to do.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do children aged 6 and 7 still cross with a grown-up?","choices":["Car speed is hard to judge, and drivers may not see small children","Because roads are always empty","Only because it is a rule at night"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about judging how fast a car is coming.","explain":"Children find car speed hard to judge, and a parked van can hide a small child. So hold a grown-up's hand to cross."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You have looked left, but not right. Can you cross?","choices":["No, look right too","Yes, one look is enough","Yes, if it is quiet"],"answer":0,"hint":"The code says look all around.","explain":"Look all around and listen. Clear on one side is not enough: a car may be coming the other way."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"At every kerb you look both ways, left and right: that is at least 2 looks. You cross {a} roads on the way to the park. How many looks is that at least?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Each road needs at least 2 looks. Add 2 for every road.","explain":"2 looks for each of {a} roads makes at least {=2*a} looks. Look all around every time, even on a quiet road.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"On a bend, why is it a poor place to cross?","choices":["You cannot see cars coming, and they cannot see you","The road is always empty there","There is never a kerb"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what is hidden around the corner.","explain":"A bend hides cars until they are close, and hides you from drivers. Pick a straight stretch or a crossing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the first step of the Green Cross Code?","choices":["Find a safer place to cross","Run across quickly","Look one way"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where you stand, before you look.","explain":"First find a safer place, such as a zebra crossing. Then stop, look, listen, wait, and walk."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"At each kerb you look left, then right, then left again. You stop at {a} kerbs. How many looks is that?","answer":"3*a","hint":"Each kerb needs 3 looks: left, right, left. Add 3 for every kerb.","explain":"3 looks at each of {a} kerbs makes {=3*a} looks. Looking all around means more than one way, every time.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You wait at the kerb. {a} cars pass from the left and {b} cars pass from the right. How many cars did you wait for?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the cars from both sides. You wait for every one.","explain":"{a} from the left and {b} from the right is {=a+b} cars. You cross only when nothing is coming either way.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The road is clear on your left. A car is coming on your right. What do you do?","choices":["Wait for the car to pass","Cross now","Run fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clear on one side is not enough.","explain":"Wait. A car is still coming from the right. Cross only when nothing is coming either way."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is a safer place to cross?","choices":["A zebra crossing","A gap between two parked cars","A bend in the road"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where drivers can see you, and where you can see them.","explain":"A zebra crossing is made for crossing. Parked cars hide you, and a bend hides cars coming round the corner."}],"explain":{"kid":["First, find a safer place to cross. A zebra crossing or a crossing with a green figure is better than a gap between parked cars or a bend.","Stop just before the kerb. Look all around and listen. Clear on one side is not enough: a car may be coming the other way.","If a car is coming, wait for it to pass. Then walk straight across. Keep looking and listening as you go. Do not run.","A parked van can hide you from a driver. Step out to a clear place. Far-away cars can still come quickly if they are going fast, so hold a grown-up's hand."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. This lesson teaches the UK Green Cross Code (Department for Transport THINK!): find the safest place to cross, stop just before the kerb, look all around and listen, wait until the road is clear, then walk straight across and keep looking and listening. THINK! and RoSPA advise that children under about 8 still cross with a grown-up. Key words: kerb, look, listen, wait, zebra crossing, green figure, grown-up.","Parked cars and vans hide a small child from a driver's line of sight, so stepping out between them is a common cause of child pedestrian injuries (RoSPA). Young children also find it hard to judge how fast a car is coming and how long it will take to arrive (WHO 2008, World report on child injury prevention). That is why 'far away' is not a reliable rule, and why holding a grown-up's hand is still needed at this age.","Common mistakes: crossing between parked cars or on a bend, looking only one way, running across, stopping looking half-way, thinking 'I can see the car so the driver can see me', and thinking a far-away car is always slower to arrive than a near one. Be careful not to scare the child: the aim is a clear plan they can practise with you, not a list of dangers.","In the lab your child finds two safer places on a street, puts the five steps of the Green Cross Code in order, looks left and right then waits or crosses on three roads, drags a child out from behind a parked van until the driver's line of sight is clear, and watches a far, fast car beat a near, slow one."]},"familyTip":"On your next walk, let your child find a safer place to cross and say the steps out loud before you go: stop, look all around, listen, wait, then walk and keep looking. Talk about why you do not step out between parked cars, and hold their hand even on a quiet street.","keyIdea":"To cross the road, first find a safer place: a zebra crossing or a crossing with a green figure, not a gap between parked cars or a bend. Stop just before the kerb. Look all around and listen. Wait until no traffic is coming, then walk straight across and keep looking and listening. Children under about 8 cross with a grown-up, because car speed is hard to judge and a parked van can hide a small child from a driver.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Safer or not? Drag each place, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Safer place to cross","Not a safe place"],"items":[["Zebra crossing",0],["Between parked cars",1],["Green figure lights",0],["On a bend",1],["Crossing with a grown-up",0],["Running into the road",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagroad","caption":"Flagship lab: find safer places, put the Green Cross Code in order, look both ways, step out from behind a van, race a far fast car against a near slow one.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_8S-cyefieA":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How long do you brush your teeth?","choices":["About 2 minutes","About 10 seconds","Until it tastes minty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every part of your mouth needs a turn.","explain":"About 2 minutes, so the top and bottom, this side and that side all get brushed. A quick brush misses plaque."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"When is the best time for a sweet treat?","choices":["With a meal","A little bit all day","After the bedtime brush"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each sweet snack starts a new acid attack.","explain":"With a meal. Teeth are already having one acid attack at the meal, so the treat does not start a new one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"When do you brush your teeth?","choices":["Last thing at night and one other time","Only when they feel dirty","Once a week"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twice a day, and one of those is at bedtime.","explain":"Twice a day: last thing at night and one other time, such as in the morning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"You eat 3 meals. You also have {a} sweet snacks between meals. Each meal and each sweet snack makes an acid attack. How many acid attacks is that?","answer":"3+a","hint":"Start at 3 for the meals, then count on 1 for each sweet snack.","explain":"3 meals and {a} sweet snacks make {=3+a} acid attacks. Moving the sweets to mealtimes would make it just 3.","params":{"a":[0,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do you not rinse after brushing?","choices":["Rinsing washes the fluoride off","Water is bad for teeth","Rinsing makes teeth dirty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what is in the toothpaste.","explain":"Toothpaste has fluoride, which keeps teeth strong. Rinsing washes it away, so just spit."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How do you brush?","choices":["Gently, in small circles","As hard as I can","Very fast, side to side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about your soft gums, and what hard scrubbing would do to them.","explain":"Gently, in small circles, along the gums too. Scrubbing hard can hurt your gums and does not clean better."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You brush the outside of {a} teeth. Then you brush the inside of the same {a} teeth. How many sides did you brush?","answer":"a+a","hint":"Add the outsides and the insides: the same number twice.","explain":"{a} outsides and {a} insides make {=a+a} sides. Do not forget the chewing tops too!","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How much toothpaste does a baby under 3 need?","choices":["A tiny smear","A pea-sized blob","A long stripe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Babies need less than you.","explain":"A tiny smear for a baby under 3. A pea-sized blob for children aged 3 to 6."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which parts of each tooth do you brush?","choices":["The outside, the inside and the top","Only the front that shows","Only the teeth at the back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the side next to your tongue, and the part you chew with.","explain":"Brush every side: the outside, the inside next to your tongue, and the chewing tops. Plaque hides on all of them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You brush your teeth 2 times every day. How many times is that in {a} days?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Count in twos: 2 for each day. Add 2 for every day.","explain":"2 times a day for {a} days makes {=2*a} brushes: one at bedtime and one other time, every day.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{a} teeth have plaque on the outside and {b} teeth have plaque on the inside. How many places do you need to brush?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the outsides, then count on the insides.","explain":"{a} outsides and {b} insides make {=a+b} places to brush. The inside counts just as much as the outside.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You have finished brushing. What do you do next?","choices":["Spit it out, and do not rinse","Rinse with lots of water","Have a drink of juice"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fluoride works best if it stays on your teeth.","explain":"Spit the toothpaste out but do not rinse, so the fluoride stays on your teeth. After the bedtime brush, only water."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How much toothpaste do you put on your brush?","choices":["A blob the size of a pea","A long stripe","As much as fits"],"answer":0,"hint":"More toothpaste does not clean better.","explain":"A pea-sized blob is right for children aged 3 to 6. A baby under 3 needs only a tiny smear."}],"explain":{"kid":["Sticky plaque grows on your teeth every day. It is made of germs. Brushing takes it off. Brush the outside, the inside next to your tongue, and the tops you chew with. The front is only one side.","Use a blob of toothpaste the size of a pea. More does not clean better, and a baby needs only a tiny smear. Brush gently in small circles for about 2 minutes, so every part of your mouth gets a turn: top and bottom, this side and that side.","When you finish, spit the toothpaste out. Do not rinse with water. The toothpaste has fluoride in it, and fluoride keeps your teeth strong if you leave it there.","Sweet things make acid in your mouth. Each time you eat or drink something sweet, the acid works on your teeth for up to an hour. So have sweet things with meals, not a bit all day. After the bedtime brush, only water."],"adult":["Ages 4 to 5. This follows NHS advice for children aged 3 to 6. Brush at least twice a day for about 2 minutes: last thing at night and one other time. Use a pea-sized blob of fluoride toothpaste (1,000 to 1,500 ppm fluoride). Then spit it out and do not rinse, because rinsing washes the fluoride away. Children under 3 need only a smear. Let your child brush, and watch and help. The NHS says most children can brush on their own from about 7. Key words: plaque, fluoride, toothpaste, gums, spit, rinse.","Plaque is a sticky layer of germs. When we eat or drink something sugary, the germs turn the sugar into acid. Teeth are then under acid attack for up to about an hour (Oral Health Foundation; NHS dental diet leaflets). So how often matters, not just how much. Three sweets eaten with lunch make one attack. The same sweets nibbled all afternoon make several. That is why sweet food and drinks are best kept to mealtimes, with water or milk in between. After the bedtime brush, only water.","Common mistakes: brushing only the front teeth, a long stripe of toothpaste, a quick ten-second brush, scrubbing hard, rinsing straight after brushing, and juice or milk after the bedtime brush. Be careful not to scare your child about holes in teeth. The aim is a calm routine you do together every night.","In the lab your child scrubs plaque off the outside, inside and tops of four teeth. They squeeze a pea-sized blob for themselves and a smear for a baby. They brush four parts of the mouth until a timer reaches 2 minutes. They put bedtime brushing in order and see rinsing wash the fluoride off. Last, they move juice, sweets and a biscuit to mealtimes, which cuts the acid attacks from 6 to 3. In the lab, each meal counts as one attack, a simple model of the real thing."]},"familyTip":"Brush together tonight with an egg timer or a 2-minute song. Let your child brush first, then you finish off and check the insides and the tops. Squeeze the pea-sized blob together, and make 'spit, don't rinse' the last step before bed, with only water after it.","keyIdea":"Brush your teeth twice a day: last thing at night and one other time. Brush for about 2 minutes, gently, in small circles. Clean every tooth on the outside, the inside and the chewing tops, and along the gums. Use a pea-sized blob of fluoride toothpaste. Spit it out, but do not rinse with water, so the fluoride stays on your teeth. Keep sweet food and drinks to mealtimes, because each sugary snack starts an acid attack on teeth that lasts up to an hour.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Good for teeth or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Good for my teeth","Not good for my teeth"],"items":[["Brush for 2 minutes",0],["Rinse after brushing",1],["A pea of toothpaste",0],["Sweets all day long",1],["Brush at bedtime",0],["Brush only the front",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbrush","caption":"Flagship lab: scrub plaque off every side, squeeze a pea and a smear, brush four parts for 2 minutes, put bedtime brushing in order, move treats to mealtimes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_h-z88yf9Pn":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Tom and Jas fall out once over a game and both shout. Is it bullying?","choices":["Yes, shouting is always bullying","No, it was once, and they are equals","Yes, if anyone heard it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Bullying is again and again, with more power.","explain":"A one-off fall-out between equals is not bullying. It is still worth sorting out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"For {n} days, a group sends Sami mean messages in a game chat. What is the best word?","choices":["Peer pressure","Cyberbullying","A dilemma"],"answer":1,"hint":"Where is it happening, and is it again and again?","explain":"It is repeated, on purpose and online for {n} days, so it is cyberbullying.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is a bystander?","choices":["Someone who sees bullying and does nothing","Someone who stops bullying","Someone who starts bullying"],"answer":0,"hint":"A bystander just stands by.","explain":"A bystander sees it and does nothing. Doing nothing lets bullying carry on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ava finds £{n} on the floor. Nobody saw it drop. She hands it in. What does this show?","choices":["Peer pressure","Integrity","Consent"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which word means doing right when no one is watching?","explain":"Nobody would know if she kept the £{n}, so handing it in shows integrity.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is a responsibility, not a right?","choices":["To be safe","To learn","Not to stop others learning"],"answer":2,"hint":"A responsibility is something you should do for others.","explain":"Being safe and learning are rights. Not stopping others learning is the responsibility that protects their right."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Lena can tell the teacher who broke the window and upset her friend, or say nothing and feel bad. What is this?","choices":["A dilemma","Bullying","A right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every choice here has a downside.","explain":"A dilemma is a hard choice where every option has a downside."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Before posting, Raj asks his {n} friends in the photo, and they all say yes. What did he get?","choices":["Peer pressure","Consent","A dilemma"],"answer":1,"hint":"He asked first and got a clear yes.","explain":"Asking all {n} friends and getting a clear yes is consent.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which upstander action is safest when you see bullying in the playground?","choices":["Fight the bully","Tell a teacher you trust","Laugh so they leave you alone"],"answer":1,"hint":"An upstander acts, and stays safe.","explain":"Telling a trusted adult is a safe way to act. You never have to fight, and laughing along is joining in."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"For {n} weeks, an older girl takes Ruby's snack and laughs. Ruby cannot make her stop. Which citizenship word names this: bullying, a fall-out or an accident?","choices":["A one-off fall-out","Bullying","An accident"],"answer":1,"hint":"Is it again and again, on purpose, and hard to stop?","explain":"It has gone on for {n} weeks, on purpose, and Ruby cannot stop it, so it is bullying.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Kai sees Leo left out and teased. Kai goes and tells a teacher. What is Kai?","choices":["A bystander","An upstander","A bully"],"answer":1,"hint":"Did Kai do something safe to help?","explain":"Kai acted in a safe way by telling an adult, so he is an upstander. A bystander does nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} friends tell Ella to share a photo of Mo. Mo has not said yes. What should Ella think about?","choices":["Whether she will get caught","Consent: Mo has not said yes","Whether the photo is funny"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which word means asking first and getting a clear yes?","explain":"Even with {n} friends pushing, the photo is Mo's to agree to. Without his yes, Ella should not share it.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You have the right to be safe at school. Which responsibility goes with it?","choices":["Not to hurt or scare others","To get the best seat","To win every game"],"answer":0,"hint":"Everyone else has the same right as you.","explain":"Others have the right to be safe too, so your responsibility is not to hurt or scare them."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which post shows good digital citizenship?","choices":["A photo of a friend, after she said yes","A joke about a classmate's hair","Your school name and street"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it safe, kind and fair?","explain":"Asking first is fair, and the post is kind and safe. The joke is unkind, and the address is not safe."}],"explain":{"kid":["Bullying is hurting someone again and again, on purpose. It is hard for them to make it stop. A fall-out between two friends, just once, is not bullying. An accident is not bullying. It is still worth fixing. Bullying online, on phones or games, is called cyberbullying.","A bystander sees bullying and does nothing. An upstander sees it and does something safe. You can tell a teacher or ask the child to play. You never have to fight. Telling an adult is not telling tales. It is how bullying stops.","Peer pressure is when friends push you to do something. A good reason helps you say no. Consent means you ask first and get a clear yes. A no means no. Integrity means you do the right thing even when no one sees. \"Nobody will find out\" is not a reason. It is just about getting caught.","A right is something every person should have, like being safe. A responsibility is something you should do, so others keep their rights too. Ethical means right and fair. A dilemma is a hard choice where every choice has a bad side. Digital citizenship means being safe, kind and fair online."],"adult":["This topic gives children aged 7 to 11 the words for right and wrong with other people: bullying, cyberbullying, bystander, upstander, peer pressure, consent, integrity, rights, responsibilities, ethical, dilemma and digital citizenship. Naming a situation helps a child decide what to do and ask for help.","The lab uses five short scenes. Learners set three switches to test whether a story is bullying. They move themselves to a choice in a playground. They pull a peer-pressure rope back with real reasons. They balance rights with responsibilities and fix a post before it goes online.","Common mistakes: calling every mean moment bullying, when bullying is repeated, deliberate and involves a power imbalance. Many children think a bystander is not involved, or that an upstander must confront the bully, when telling a trusted adult counts. Some treat a right as permission to do anything, or think a photo is fine to share without asking.","At home, use the words about real events, books and shows. Ask what an upstander could do here, or whether someone said yes first. Agree together what your child will do if they see bullying online: save it, block, and tell you."]},"familyTip":"Pick a story from a book or TV show and ask: who was a bystander, who was an upstander, and was there a dilemma? Then agree one safe thing your child would do in that spot.","keyIdea":"Bullying is hurting someone again and again, on purpose, when it is hard for them to stop it; online it is cyberbullying. A bystander sees it and does nothing, and an upstander acts in a safe way. Peer pressure is feeling pushed by friends. Consent means asking and getting a clear yes. Integrity is doing right when no one is watching. Every right comes with a responsibility. Ethical means right and fair, and a dilemma is a hard choice where every option has a downside. Digital citizenship is being safe, kind and fair online.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Bystander or upstander? Ask: did they do something safe to help?","opts":{"bins":["Upstander","Bystander"],"items":[["Tells a teacher",0],["Asks the child to play",0],["Reports a mean post",0],["Walks past",1],["Looks away and says nothing",1],["Scrolls past a mean post",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcit","caption":"Flagship lab: test stories with bullying switches, choose bystander or upstander, pull back peer pressure, balance rights and fix a post.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sRM9-k3NOq":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a balanced budget?","choices":["Income minus spending minus saving equals zero, so every pound has a job","Spending is lower than last month","You never buy any wants"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about every pound having a planned use.","explain":"A balanced budget plans every pound: spending plus saving equals income, so nothing is left without a job."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A £{p} snack every school day for 190 days. How much is that over the year, in pounds?","answer":"p*190","hint":"Multiply the cost by the number of days.","explain":"£{p} × 190 = £{=p*190} a year.","params":{"p":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why does paying yourself first work better than saving what is left?","choices":["Money left in your account tends to get spent","Banks charge you less","It earns double interest"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to money that sits in your current account?","explain":"Money in a current account tends to get spent. Moving savings on payday takes the choice away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Essential costs are £{e}00 a month. About how much should an emergency fund of three months hold, in pounds?","answer":"e*300","hint":"Multiply one month of essentials by three.","explain":"An emergency fund covers about three months of essential costs. 3 × £{e}00 = £{=e*300}, so a broken boiler or a lost shift does not push you into debt.","params":{"e":[6,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is a want rather than a need?","choices":["Takeaway on Friday nights","Food shopping","Bus fare to work"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could you live and get to work without it?","explain":"A takeaway is a want; food shopping and bus fare to work are needs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Jade saves £50 a month for 2 years at 4% AER. Why is her interest about £46, not £96?","choices":["Each £50 only earns from the day it goes in","The bank keeps half","Interest is only paid in the first year"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the last payment earn for two years?","explain":"Early payments earn for nearly two years, later ones for only a few months, so the interest is about £46."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your phone screen breaks and the repair is £120. Which plan protects you best?","choices":["An emergency fund you set up in advance","Pay on a credit card and worry later","Skip rent this month"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which choice avoids borrowing and missing a need?","explain":"An emergency fund covers the surprise without debt or missing a need."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A budget shows £60 with no job and savings of £0. What is the best fix?","choices":["Give the £60 a job, such as saving","Leave it for the weekend","Spend it now before it disappears"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every pound should have a planned use.","explain":"Give it a job. Planning the £60 as saving makes the budget balance and builds a buffer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Mia moves £{n}0 to savings on payday every month. After a year she has saved…","choices":["£{=n*120}, before any interest","£{n}0","Nothing, because she spent it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twelve months of the same payment.","explain":"£{n}0 × 12 = £{=n*120}, plus any interest.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a need for someone who works full time?","choices":["Rent for their room","A second streaming service","New trainers when the old pair is fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"A need keeps you housed, fed, safe or able to work.","explain":"Rent is a need because it keeps a roof over your head. The others are wants."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A drink costs £{p} and you buy one every day for a 30-day month. How much do you spend in pounds?","answer":"p*30","hint":"Multiply the daily cost by the number of days.","explain":"£{p} × 30 = £{=p*30} for the month.","params":{"p":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Take-home pay is £1,000. Rent is £450, food is £{f}0 and travel is £100. How much is left for fun and saving, in pounds?","answer":"1000-450-f*10-100","hint":"Take each cost away from the take-home pay.","explain":"£1,000 − £450 − £{f}0 − £100 = £{=1000-450-f*10-100}.","params":{"f":[12,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Leo wants to save £{n}0 a month. He saves whatever is left at the end of each month and usually saves almost nothing. What is the best change?","choices":["Move £{n}0 to savings on payday by standing order","Wait for a month when he spends less","Borrow on a card when he runs short"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which option happens before the money can be spent?","explain":"Paying yourself first moves £{n}0 out on payday, so it cannot drift into spending. Borrowing makes next month harder.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"£200 sits in a savings account at 5% AER for one year. How much interest does it earn?","choices":["£10","£100","£205"],"answer":0,"hint":"5% means 5 pence for every pound.","explain":"5% of £200 is £10. The total after one year is £210."}],"explain":{"kid":["A budget is a plan for your money before you spend it. Start with needs: things that keep you housed, fed, safe and able to work. Then plan saving. Wants come out of what is left.","Small costs add up. A £4.50 drink every day is £135 a month and over £1,600 a year. To see a small cost clearly, times it by how often you pay it.","Pay yourself first. Money left in your account tends to get spent, so move your saving on payday, before you spend. A standing order makes it happen without you thinking.","Build an emergency fund. Aim for about three months of your essential costs. Then a broken phone or a big bill does not push you into debt. Savings accounts pay interest, so the money grows the longer you leave it."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 14 to 16, covers the core of a personal budget: income, needs, wants and saving, all planned before the money is spent. A balanced budget gives every pound a job, so income minus spending minus saving is zero. Learners use take-home pay, not salary, because tax and National Insurance come out first.","Behavioural research explains why pay yourself first works. People treat money in a current account as money to spend, and saving what is left often leaves very little. Moving savings on payday by standing order removes the choice. Automatic schemes like workplace pension enrolment raise saving for the same reason.","Common mistakes: treating every cost as a need, ignoring small repeated costs, and thinking interest is simply the rate times all the money paid in. With £50 a month for two years at 4% AER, the interest is about £46, not £96, because each payment only earns from the day it arrives. MoneyHelper suggests an emergency fund of about three months of essential outgoings.","In the lab, learners sort costs into needs and wants and balance a £1,200 monthly budget for a 19-year-old apprentice. They tap a month of £4.50 drinks and run six months of saving what is left against paying yourself first. Then they grow £50 a month at 4% AER, switching to 0% to compare."]},"familyTip":"Show your teenager one real monthly bill and one bank statement (or a made-up one). Together, mark each line as a need, a want or saving, then add up the small repeated costs for a month.","keyIdea":"A budget gives every pound a job before the month starts: needs first, then saving, then wants. Small regular costs add up to big yearly ones. Saving works best when it comes out first, on payday, and an emergency fund of about three months of essential costs stops one bad surprise turning into debt. Interest pays you for leaving money in, and it builds over time.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fixed cost, flexible cost or saving? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fixed cost","Flexible cost","Saving"],"items":[["Rent",0],["Phone contract",0],["Food shopping",1],["Takeaways",1],["Emergency fund",2],["Payday standing order to savings",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbudget","caption":"Flagship lab: sort needs and wants, balance a £1,200 budget, see small costs add up, pay yourself first and watch interest grow.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_VD2nIGUEI-":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Your ball rolls into the road. What do you do?","choices":["Stop and tell a grown-up","Run and get it fast","Wait in the road for it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cars are fast. Who should get the ball?","explain":"Stop and tell a grown-up. Cars are fast and may not see you, so a grown-up gets the ball."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one is hot and can burn you?","choices":["A lit candle","A teddy","An apple"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has a flame that can burn?","explain":"A lit candle is hot and can burn. A teddy and an apple are OK to touch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ben says, \"I only hold hands by the road when Dad is looking.\" Is that right?","choices":["No, cars are fast every time","Yes, rules are just for Dad","Yes, if the road is small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are cars slower when Dad is not looking?","explain":"Ben is not right. The rule keeps him safe from cars every time, whoever is watching."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Dad has cooked {n} pancakes. The pan is on the cooker. What do you do?","choices":["Look from far away","Touch the pan","Climb up to see"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pan on the cooker can be very hot.","explain":"Look from far away. A pan on the cooker can burn you, even when you cannot see a flame.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do we hold hands by the road?","choices":["Cars are big and fast","So we can go faster","Because it is fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is on the road that could hurt us?","explain":"We hold hands because cars are big and fast. A grown-up can keep you on the path."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"It is {n} minutes to snack time. You are inside. How do you get to the table?","choices":["Walk","Run as fast as you can","Slide on the floor"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which way stops you tripping and bumping?","explain":"Walk. Running or sliding inside can make you trip and bump, even when you are in a hurry.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do we stay with our grown-up at the park?","choices":["So we do not get lost","So they can carry us","Because parks are boring"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could happen if you wander away?","explain":"We stay close so we do not get lost. Our grown-up can see us and help if we need it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which rule keeps us safe by the road?","choices":["Stop, look and listen","Run across quickly","Close your eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do we do at the kerb before we cross?","explain":"Stop, look and listen at the kerb, holding a grown-up's hand, before crossing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You are by a busy road with your grown-up. What do you do?","choices":["Hold their hand","Run ahead","Walk on your own"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cars are big and fast. Who keeps you safe by the road?","explain":"Hold your grown-up's hand. Cars are big and fast, and drivers might not see a small child."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} toys on the playroom floor. Kit wants to get to the door. What is safest?","choices":["Walk and step round the toys","Run fast to the door","Jump over the toys"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can happen if you run past toys on the floor?","explain":"Walking is safest. If Kit runs, Kit can trip on the toys and bump. Walking gives time to see them.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The kettle clicked off a minute ago. There is no steam now. Is it safe to touch?","choices":["No, it can still be hot","Yes, no steam means cold","Yes, if you are quick"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you always see when something is hot?","explain":"No. A kettle stays hot for a long time after the steam stops. Keep away and let a grown-up use it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia and her mum are in a big shop with {n} aisles. Mia sees the toys. What should she do?","choices":["Stay with her mum","Go to the toys on her own","Hide behind a shelf"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could happen in a big, busy shop if Mia goes off alone?","explain":"Mia should stay with her mum. In a big shop she could get lost. She can ask Mum to come and look at the toys with her.","params":{"n":[4,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why do we walk, not run, inside?","choices":["So we do not trip and bump","Because running is bad","So we get there first"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can happen when you run in a room full of things?","explain":"We walk inside so we do not trip on things or bump into people and furniture."}],"explain":{"kid":["By the road, hold hands with your grown-up. Cars are big and fast.","Inside, we walk. If we run, we can trip on toys and bump our heads.","Hot things can burn. A kettle can be hot with no steam. Keep away.","In the shop, stay with your grown-up. Then you will not get lost. Every rule has a reason."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 3 to 4, is about a few simple safety rules: hold hands near roads, walk indoors, keep away from hot things and stay close in busy places. Your child also learns to say why each rule matters. It leads on to everyday safety awareness and to safe choices made without a reminder.","Children of this age keep a rule much better when they know its reason. \"Hold my hand\" works better as \"Hold my hand, because cars are big and fast and drivers might not see you.\" Short reasons that name the danger (cars, tripping, burns, getting lost) work best. Practise the rule in the real place. At the kerb, stop, look and listen before every crossing, even on quiet roads.","Common mistakes: thinking a kettle, iron or pan is safe once the steam or flame has gone (they stay hot for a long time), running after a ball into the road, thinking a rule only counts when a grown-up is watching, and seeing rules as a punishment, not protection. Young children also cannot judge how fast cars are going, so they need a hand to hold, not just a warning.","In the lab your child holds hands and waits for two cars to pass before crossing. Kit trips when running indoors and gets to the door safely when walking. Then your child sorts a teapot and a candle from a teddy and an apple, matches each rule to its reason, and picks the safe choice in three quick moments."]},"familyTip":"On your next walk, let your child be the \"safety boss\" at each kerb: they tell you to stop, look and listen, and say why. At home, point to the kettle after it clicks off and ask, \"Hot or OK to touch?\"","keyIdea":"Some rules are there to stop us getting hurt. Hold a grown-up's hand by the road, because cars are big and fast. Walk, do not run, inside, so we do not trip and bump. Keep away from hot things like cookers, kettles and candles, because they can burn, even when you cannot see steam or fire. Stay with your grown-up in busy places so you do not get lost. Knowing why a rule matters makes it easier to keep.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Safe or not safe? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Safe","Not safe"],"items":[["hold hands by the road",0],["walk inside",0],["stay with a grown-up",0],["run into the road",1],["touch the cooker",1],["run off in the shop",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsafe","caption":"Flagship lab: hold hands and wait for the cars, walk past the toys, sort hot things, match each rule to its reason, then pick the safe choice.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uTKgmWqSoI":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a consequence?","choices":["What happens because of a choice","A kind of game","Only a punishment"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think: choice, then what happens next.","explain":"A consequence is what happens because of a choice. It can be good, like a happy friend, or bad, like a fall."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why do we have a rule to hold hands by the road?","choices":["To keep us safe from cars","To stop us having fun","Because grown-ups are bored"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could happen near a busy road?","explain":"The rule keeps us safe. Cars are fast, and holding hands stops you running into the road."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A friendly man you do not know says: come and see my puppy. What do you do?","choices":["Say no and tell a trusted adult","Go with him","Keep it a secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do you know him? Is he a trusted adult?","explain":"Say no and tell a trusted adult. Friendly is not the same as trusted. Never go with a stranger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Maya feeds her {n} fish every morning without being asked. What is she?","choices":["Responsible","Unfair","Unsafe"],"answer":0,"hint":"She does her job and looks after her pets.","explain":"Maya is responsible. She looks after all {n} fish and does her job every day.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You leave your coat at the park. It is {n} degrees and you are cold. What is being cold?","choices":["A consequence","A rule","Being honest"],"answer":0,"hint":"It happened because of what you did.","explain":"Being cold is a consequence. It happened because you left your coat behind, and {n} degrees is chilly.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You tell the truth about a mistake. What usually happens?","choices":["People trust you and help fix it","You always get in big trouble","Nothing ever happens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Honest words help people trust you.","explain":"People trust you and help you fix it. Being honest is right, even when it feels hard."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is a safe choice?","choices":["Wear a helmet on my bike","Ride in the road with no helmet","Climb on the roof"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one stops you getting hurt?","explain":"Wearing a helmet is safe. It protects your head if you fall. The others could hurt you."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You share your {n} stickers with a friend. Your friend smiles. What is the smile?","choices":["A consequence","A rule","A secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"It happened because of your choice.","explain":"The smile is a consequence. It happened because you chose to share your {n} stickers. Good choices have consequences too.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"You broke a cup. Which words are honest?","choices":["I broke it. I am sorry.","The dog did it.","It was broken before."],"answer":0,"hint":"Honest means telling what really happened.","explain":"\"I broke it. I am sorry\" is honest. Blaming the dog or saying it was broken before is not true."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} children want the swing. They each get a turn. What word is that?","choices":["Fair","Unsafe","Honest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Everyone gets the same go.","explain":"It is fair. All {n} children get a turn, with no favourites.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Who is a trusted adult?","choices":["My teacher","A stranger in the park","Someone online I do not know"],"answer":0,"hint":"A trusted adult is someone you know, who keeps you safe.","explain":"Your teacher is a trusted adult. You know them and they help keep you safe. A stranger is not, even if they seem kind."}],"explain":{"kid":["A choice is when you pick what to do. Keep the ball, or give it back? A consequence is what happens next. It happens because of your choice. Good choices have consequences too. You give the ball back. Your friend is happy. You grab the cookie. Your brother is cross.","Honest means you tell the truth. Say I broke it, not the cat did it. Then you can fix it with help.","A rule tells us what to do. We hold hands by the road, so we are safe. Fair means everyone gets a turn. Responsible means you do your jobs, like feeding your pet.","Feel unsafe? Tell a trusted adult. That is a grown-up you know, like your teacher, your mum or your grandma. A stranger is not one, even a kind one."],"adult":["This topic gives five to eight year olds the words for making decisions: choice, consequence, rule, safe, fair, honest, responsible, right and wrong, and trusted adult. Naming the ideas helps children stop and think before they act, and gives them words to talk a choice through with you.","The key link is between a choice and its consequence. Children often think consequence means punishment. Show them that good choices have consequences too, such as a happy friend or easier reading. Ask: what might happen next if you do that?","Common mistakes: thinking only bad choices have consequences; thinking a rule is just to stop fun, not to keep people safe and fair; and thinking telling the truth always gets you in trouble. A key safety slip is thinking that a friendly person is a trusted adult. A trusted adult is someone the child knows who helps keep them safe, such as a parent, a carer, a teacher or a police officer. They should never go with a stranger, however kind.","In the lab your child walks down both roads of a choice to see each consequence, sorts consequences into good and bad, and picks the honest words after a mistake. Then they bring the trusted adults into a safe circle and match the words fair, honest, rule, safe and responsible to pictures."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, play 'What happens next?': describe a small choice from the day (sharing a toy, leaving a coat at school) and ask what the consequence was. Together, make a list of your child's trusted adults and say their names out loud.","keyIdea":"A choice is when you pick what to do. A consequence is what happens because of that choice, and it can be good or bad. A rule tells everyone what to do so we stay safe and are fair. Honest means telling the truth, even after a mistake. Responsible means doing your jobs and looking after things. A trusted adult is a grown-up you know who helps keep you safe; if something feels unsafe, tell one.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a safe choice or an unsafe choice? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🛡️ Safe","⚠️ Unsafe"],"items":[["Wear a helmet on my bike",0],["Run across the road",1],["Tell my teacher I feel scared",0],["Go off with a stranger",1],["Hold hands by the road",0],["Climb on the cooker",1],["Ask before I touch medicine",0],["Keep a secret that scares me",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdecide","caption":"Flagship lab: walk down both roads of a choice, sort consequences, pick honest words, bring trusted adults into the circle and name each picture.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XQPZwsjr3_":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What can Pip the dog eat?","choices":["Her own dog food","Chocolate","Your sweets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Some of our treats make dogs ill.","explain":"Dogs eat dog food. Chocolate and sweets can make a dog very poorly."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Dad has lots of heavy shopping. What do you say?","choices":["Can I help?","Not my job","I want a biscuit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Good helpers ask before they jump in.","explain":"\"Can I help?\" is kind. Dad can give you a small bag to carry."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"There are {n} blocks on the floor. Mia puts {k} in the box. How many are still on the floor?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take away the ones Mia put in the box.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} blocks on the floor. Keep tidying!","params":{"n":[4,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which job is right for you?","choices":["Washing the carrots","Chopping with a sharp knife","Lifting the hot pan"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one can not cut or burn you?","explain":"Washing carrots is safe. Sharp knives and hot pans are grown-up jobs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your friend spills juice. What do you do?","choices":["Get a cloth and help","Laugh at them","Walk away"],"answer":0,"hint":"What would make your friend feel better?","explain":"Helping wipe it up is kind and makes the mess go away fast."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You push all the toys under the sofa. Is that tidy?","choices":["No, they need to go home","Yes, it looks tidy","Yes, toys like it there"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you find them next time?","explain":"Hidden is not tidy. Toys go back to their own home so we can find them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Four children each have one cup. {n} more friends come. How many cups now?","answer":"4+n","hint":"Start at 4 cups and count on one for each new friend.","explain":"4 and {n} more makes {=4+n} cups, one for each child.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Who can help tidy up?","choices":["Everyone, me too","Only grown-ups","Only big kids"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can small hands lift a toy?","explain":"Everyone can help, even little ones. When we all help, it is done fast."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Tidy up time! Where does a book go?","choices":["On the shelf","Under the sofa","On the floor"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where can you find it next time?","explain":"Books go back on the shelf. Then you can find them, and no one steps on them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} toys on the floor. You put them all in the toy box. How many toys are in the box?","answer":"n","hint":"Count each toy as you put it in.","explain":"You put in every toy, so {n} toys are in the box. The floor is clear.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why do we tidy toys off the floor?","choices":["So no one trips","So the toys get lost","So we never play again"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can happen if you step on a toy?","explain":"Toys on the floor can make someone trip and fall. Tidying keeps everyone safe."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} people sit down for snack. You give one cup to each person. How many cups do you need?","answer":"n","hint":"One cup for each person. Count the people.","explain":"{n} people need {n} cups, one each.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every toy has a home. Blocks go in the toy box. Books go on the shelf. Coats go on the peg. When we put things back, we can find them next time.","Toys on the floor can trip us. Ouch! A tidy floor keeps everyone safe. Pushing toys under the sofa is not tidy. Put them in their home.","You can help at home. Set out one cup for each person. Give the dog her own dinner and some water. Wash the carrots with Gran.","Ask, \"Can I help?\" Pick a small job. Sharp knives, hot pans and heavy bags are for grown-ups. When we all help, the job is quick and everyone is happy."],"adult":["Development Matters (2023) covers this in Personal, Social and Emotional Development. At 3 and 4, children develop their sense of responsibility and membership of a community. They also become more independent in meeting their own care needs. Small jobs lead on to the Managing Self early learning goal at the end of Reception.","Children tidy best when each thing has one clear home, with a picture label on the box or shelf. Say the reason out loud: so we can find it, so no one trips. Setting a cup for each person is early maths too. It uses one-to-one correspondence. Real jobs done next to an adult build more pride than praise alone.","Common mistakes: redoing the job straight away, so the child learns their help is not wanted. Letting 'tidy' mean hiding things. Offering jobs that are too big, sharp or hot. Be careful with pets. Chocolate, grapes and raisins are poisonous to dogs. Children give only the pet's own dinner, and an adult measures it.","In the lab your child puts six things in their homes, and finds that under the sofa is refused. They clear four toys so Teddy can walk to the door without tripping. They set one cup for each of four people. They give Pip her dinner and water but not chocolate. Then they pick kind jobs of the right size in three helping scenes."]},"familyTip":"Give each kind of toy one home with a picture on the box. At tidy-up time, say the reason (\"so no one trips\") and work alongside your child for the first few minutes, then let them finish one small job alone and leave it as they did it.","keyIdea":"Helping means doing small jobs that make things better for everyone: putting toys back in their own home, setting out a cup for each person, giving a pet its own dinner and water. We tidy so things can be found again and so no one trips. Good helpers ask \"Can I help?\" and pick a job that is the right size, leaving sharp, hot and heavy jobs to grown-ups.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"A small job for me, or a job for a grown-up? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🙋 I can help","🧑 Grown-up job"],"items":[["putting toys in the box",0],["setting out the cups",0],["watering a plant",0],["feeding the fish with a grown-up",0],["chopping with a sharp knife",1],["lifting a hot pan",1],["carrying a heavy box",1],["plugging in the kettle",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtidy","caption":"Flagship lab: put things in their homes, clear the path so no one trips, set a cup for each person, feed the dog safely and pick a right-sized helping job.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_3sQ7ndzhV0":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is compound interest?","choices":["Interest charged on the principal and on earlier interest","Interest charged only on the amount first borrowed","A fee for paying late"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the £1,000 that grew to about £1,950 in three years.","explain":"Compound interest is charged on the principal and on interest already added, so the debt grows faster each year."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why does paying only the minimum on a credit card take so long?","choices":["The minimum shrinks as the balance shrinks, and most of it pays interest","The bank adds a new fee every month","The APR goes up each year"],"answer":0,"hint":"The minimum is the interest plus 1% of the balance.","explain":"Most of each minimum payment covers interest, and the minimum falls as the balance falls, so £1,500 takes over 22 years."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"£{p}00 is borrowed at 10% a year, compounded yearly, with nothing repaid. What is owed after 2 years, in pounds?","answer":"p*121","hint":"Multiply by 1.1 for each year: 1.1 × 1.1 = 1.21.","explain":"£{p}00 × 1.21 = £{=p*121}. The second year charges interest on the first year's interest too.","params":{"p":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"You borrow £1,000 and repay {n} monthly payments of £{m}. What is the cost of credit, in pounds?","answer":"n*m-1000","hint":"Total repaid minus the amount borrowed.","explain":"{n} × £{m} = £{=n*m}. Take away the £1,000 borrowed: the cost of credit is £{=n*m-1000}.","params":{"n":[12,24],"m":[90,120]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is the best way to compare two loans?","choices":["Compare the APR and the total amount repayable","Pick the lowest monthly payment","Pick the longest term"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember Loan A and Loan B in the lab.","explain":"The APR and the total repayable show the real cost. A lower monthly payment over a longer term usually costs more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What can happen if you miss a buy now, pay later payment?","choices":["Late fees and a mark on your credit file","Nothing, because it is not real credit","The shop takes the item back and the debt ends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Most of these deals are now regulated credit.","explain":"Missed payments can bring late fees and can be reported to credit reference agencies, which affects future borrowing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Two graduates both earn £35,000. One owes £45,000 on Plan 5 and one owes £60,000. Who repays more each month?","choices":["They repay the same","The one who owes £60,000","The one who owes £45,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"What decides the Plan 5 repayment?","explain":"Plan 5 repayments are 9% of earnings above £25,000, so both pay £900 a year, about £75 a month."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is a credit file?","choices":["A record of how you have used credit, checked by lenders","A list of your savings","A bank's list of interest rates"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lenders look at it before they lend to you.","explain":"A credit file records your borrowing and repayments. Lenders check it for phones, car finance and mortgages."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does APR stand for, and what does it show?","choices":["Annual percentage rate: the yearly cost of borrowing, including fees","Annual payment rate: the amount you repay each month","Average principal remaining: what you still owe"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a percentage that lets you compare the cost of different deals.","explain":"APR is the annual percentage rate. It shows the yearly cost of borrowing, including interest and most fees."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"On a Plan 5 student loan you repay 9% of earnings above £25,000. How much do you repay in a year on a salary of £{s},000?","answer":"(s-25)*90","hint":"Find how much of the salary is above £25,000, then take 9% of it.","explain":"£{s},000 − £25,000 = £{=(s-25)*1000}. 9% of that is £{=(s-25)*90} a year.","params":{"s":[27,45]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You make {n} buys of £60 each, all pay in 3: a third now, then a third in each of the next two months. How much is due next month, in pounds?","answer":"n*20","hint":"Each £60 buy needs £20 next month.","explain":"{n} × £20 = £{=n*20} due next month, and the same the month after.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Loan A is £99.92 a month for 36 months. Loan B is £78.73 a month for 84 months. Which costs less in total?","choices":["Loan A","Loan B","They cost the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply each monthly payment by the number of months.","explain":"Loan A totals about £3,597 and Loan B about £6,613. The lower monthly payment costs £3,016 more."}],"explain":{"kid":["Credit is borrowed money. A credit card, an overdraft, a loan and buy now, pay later are all credit. The amount you borrow is the principal. Interest is the extra you pay for borrowing it. Debt is what you still owe.","The APR is the yearly cost of borrowing as a percentage. It includes interest and most fees, so it is the fair way to compare deals. A lower APR is cheaper. Interest also compounds. Each month you pay interest on last month's interest too. Leave £1,000 on a card at 24.9% APR for three years and you owe about £1,950.","A card only asks for a small minimum payment. On £1,500 at 24.9%, the minimum takes over 22 years and costs about £2,600 in interest. Paying a fixed £100 a month clears it in 18 months for £277. With loans, look at the total you repay, not just the monthly payment. A longer term means more months of interest.","Missed payments go on your credit file. Lenders check it when you want a phone contract, a car loan or a mortgage. A Plan 5 student loan is different. You pay 9% of what you earn above £25,000. If you earn less, you pay nothing. After 40 years, what is left is written off."],"adult":["This lesson covers the money parts of PSHE Living in the Wider World at Key Stage 5. It builds on financial education in KS3 and KS4 citizenship. Pupils should use the terms credit, debt, principal, interest, APR, compound interest, minimum repayment, overdraft, credit file and deferred payment credit. They should also judge the total cost of credit.","All figures in the lab are worked out from the stated rates. A 24.9% APR is a monthly rate of about 1.87%. The minimum payment is modelled as interest plus 1% of the balance, or £5 if higher, which is the usual rule for UK cards. The loan figures use standard repayment maths. Most buy now, pay later deals became regulated by the FCA in July 2026, with checks on whether people can afford them.","Common mistakes: comparing loans on the monthly payment instead of the total repayable; thinking the minimum payment is a sensible plan; treating buy now, pay later as not being real debt; and ignoring overdraft charges. Many students also misread student loans. On Plan 5, what you owe does not change what you pay each month. It is 9% of earnings above £25,000, and interest is set by the RPI.","In the lab, your teenager drags a slider through three years of compound interest and tries five ways to clear £1,500. They reveal the full cost of two £3,000 loans and pick the cheaper one. Then they stack four pay in 3 buys against a £50 budget and slide a salary to see Plan 5 repayments."]},"familyTip":"Next time a loan, phone contract or pay in 3 offer comes up, ask your teenager to find two numbers: the APR and the total amount repayable. Then ask what happens if one payment is missed.","keyIdea":"Credit means borrowing money now and paying it back later, usually with interest. The APR shows the yearly cost of borrowing, including fees, so it lets you compare deals. Interest compounds: you pay interest on earlier interest. Paying only the minimum on a credit card can take decades, a lower monthly payment over a longer term often costs more in total, and small buy now, pay later instalments add up. A Plan 5 student loan works differently: you repay 9% of earnings above £25,000, whatever you owe.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Costly or careful? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["💸 Costly: adds interest, fees or risk","✅ Careful: keeps the cost of credit down"],"items":[["Paying only the minimum on a card",0],["Choosing a loan on the lowest monthly payment",0],["Stacking several pay in 3 buys",0],["Going over your agreed overdraft",0],["Setting a fixed payment above the minimum",1],["Comparing deals by APR and total repayable",1],["Paying the card in full each month",1],["Checking your credit file for errors",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdebt","caption":"Flagship lab: watch interest compound, beat the minimum-payment trap, compare loans by total cost, stack pay in 3 buys and slide a Plan 5 salary.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Wc6cOTQ1bA":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You find a teddy on a bench in the park. No one is there. What should you do?","choices":["Give it to a grown-up","Keep it, finders keepers","Throw it away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the teddy still belong to someone?","explain":"The teddy still belongs to someone. A grown-up can help find the owner."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Leo broke a cup by accident and told his mum. How does his mum feel about him telling her?","choices":["Glad he told the truth","Cross that he told her","She does not care"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about trust. Is it good to know the truth?","explain":"Mum is glad Leo told the truth. She can trust him, and they can fix it together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ben is sitting on his own at playtime and looks sad. What is a kind choice?","choices":["Ask Ben to play","Walk past Ben","Point at Ben and laugh"],"answer":0,"hint":"How would you feel if you were on your own?","explain":"Asking Ben to play helps him feel wanted. Walking past or laughing makes him feel worse."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Your friend says: let's take a sweet from the shop, no one will see. What do you do?","choices":["Say no","Take one","Take one for your friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it right to take something without paying, even if no one sees?","explain":"Taking without paying is stealing, even when no one sees. The right thing is to say no."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"There are {=3*k} stickers for 3 friends. To be fair, how many stickers does each friend get?","answer":"k","hint":"Share them out one at a time: one for each friend, again and again.","explain":"{=3*k} stickers shared the same between 3 friends is {k} each.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Jo broke a toy. She says her little brother did it. Why is that wrong?","choices":["It is a lie and her brother gets told off","It is fine if no one finds out","Little brothers break things"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who gets in trouble, and is it true?","explain":"Blaming her brother is a lie, and he gets told off for something he did not do."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Zara has {a} toy cars and Tom has none. How many should Zara give Tom so they each have {=a/2}?","answer":"a/2","hint":"Fair means the same. Give Tom one car at a time until both have the same.","explain":"Zara gives Tom {=a/2} cars. Then Zara has {=a/2} and Tom has {=a/2}, so it is fair.","params":{"a":[2,8]},"where":"a%2==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which one is a right choice?","choices":["Saying sorry when you hurt someone","Grabbing the best toy first","Telling a friend's secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is honest, fair or kind?","explain":"Saying sorry is honest and kind. Grabbing and telling secrets are unfair and unkind."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mia spills juice on the sofa by accident. What is the right thing to do?","choices":["Tell a grown-up","Put a cushion over it","Say the dog did it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which choice is honest and helps fix the spill?","explain":"Telling a grown-up is honest, and they can help clean it. Hiding it or blaming the dog is not honest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {a} children. To be fair, each child gets {b} grapes. How many grapes do we need?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Count {b} grapes for each child, then add them all up.","explain":"{a} children with {b} grapes each need {=a*b} grapes. Then each child has the same.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Sam wants to use Ava's new pens. What should he do first?","choices":["Ask Ava","Take them while she is out","Take just one"],"answer":0,"hint":"The pens belong to Ava. What do we do with things that are not ours?","explain":"The pens are Ava's, so Sam asks first. Taking even one without asking is not right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {=2*k} biscuits for 2 children. To be fair, how many biscuits does each child get?","answer":"k","hint":"Give one to each child, then one more to each, until the biscuits are all gone.","explain":"{=2*k} biscuits shared the same between 2 children is {k} each.","params":{"k":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Before you choose, stop and think: how will the other person feel? If Tom wants a go with the ball, giving him a turn makes him smile. Keeping it all day makes him sad.","Tell the truth, even when you made a mistake. An accident is not naughty. If you hide it or say the cat did it, people find out and trust you less. If you tell, they can help you fix it.","Some things are yours, like your teddy and your lunch. Some things belong to someone else, like a friend's crayons. Ask first. A toy you find in the park is still someone's. Give it to a grown-up.","Fair means each person gets the same. Six strawberries for three children is two each. The right thing can be hard, like saying no to a friend. It is still right, and you feel good after."],"adult":["This topic sits in the primary Relationships Education guidance for England. It asks pupils to learn why honesty and truthfulness matter, to respect other people and their things, and to be fair in friendships. PSHE plans for Key Stage 1 add the difference between right and wrong, and how choices have consequences for us and for others.","Children of five to seven are building a conscience: a sense of right and wrong that works even when no adult is watching. Four skills carry it. Take the other person's view (how will Tom feel?). Own up after an accident. Respect property: a thing is not yours just because no one is there. See fair as equal shares. Praise the choice and the reason, not only the result.","Common mistakes at this age: judging an act by the size of the damage, not the intention, so ten cups broken by accident seem worse than one broken on purpose. Thinking a lie is fine if no one finds out. Believing finders keepers. Treating fair as whatever the finder or the oldest child wants. Be careful not to punish a child who owns up to an accident, or they learn to hide things.","In the lab your child picks kind or unkind choices and watches a face change. They play out telling the truth, hiding a broken cup and blaming the cat, while Mum's trust hearts rise or fall. Then they sort six things into ask first or mine to use, share six strawberries fairly, and take the harder right path three times."]},"familyTip":"When something goes wrong at home, say thank you first when your child owns up, then fix it together. Ask: how do you think they felt? and what would be fair?","keyIdea":"A right choice is honest, fair and kind, and it thinks about how other people will feel. Tell the truth, even after an accident: hiding it or blaming someone else breaks trust. Ask before you use things that belong to someone else. Share so each person gets the same. The right thing is sometimes the harder thing, and it is still right.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Right choice or wrong choice? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👍 Right choice","👎 Wrong choice"],"items":[["Tell the truth about a spill",0],["Ask before you borrow",0],["Share the sweets the same",0],["Help a friend who fell",0],["Blame your brother",1],["Take a toy without asking",1],["Keep all the sweets",1],["Laugh when someone falls",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagright","caption":"Flagship lab: choose kindly and watch faces change, play out the truth about a broken cup, ask before you borrow, share fairly and take the harder right path.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_I9iSzpGRn5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What makes bullying different from a falling out?","choices":["It is on purpose, again and again, and hard to stop","Someone gets cross","It happens at school"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the STOP check: Several Times On Purpose.","explain":"Bullying is on purpose, repeated, and the other person has more power, so it is hard to stop. Friends can get cross once and make up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Every day Kim tells the others, \"Don't play with Ana.\" What kind of bullying is it?","choices":["Social bullying","Physical bullying","It is not bullying because no one is hit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Ana hurt in her body, or left out?","explain":"Leaving someone out on purpose, again and again, is social bullying. Bullying does not need hitting."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Leo is being bullied. A friend says, \"Don't tell, it will get worse.\" What should Leo do?","choices":["Tell a trusted grown-up","Keep it secret","Hit back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who has the power to make it stop?","explain":"A trusted grown-up can stop it. Secrets let bullying go on, and hitting back can get Leo hurt or in trouble."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Max has {k} friends who join in. With Max, how many are on the bully's side?","answer":"k+1","hint":"Count Max as well as his friends.","explain":"{k} friends and Max make {=k+1}. A group against one child is a power imbalance, so it is hard for that child to stop it alone.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You see Ella being pushed over every playtime. What does an upstander do?","choices":["Gets a grown-up and is kind to Ella","Watches and says nothing","Pushes the bully"],"answer":0,"hint":"An upstander helps in a safe way.","explain":"An upstander gets a grown-up and is kind to Ella. Watching does not help, and pushing could get someone hurt."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Jo got a mean message on {n} days. Each day there were 2 messages. How many messages should Jo save to show a grown-up?","answer":"2*n","hint":"2 messages each day, for {n} days.","explain":"2 messages on each of {n} days is {=2*n} messages. Saving them all shows the grown-up it keeps happening.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ben kicks a ball and it hits Rosa by accident. He says sorry. Is it bullying?","choices":["No, it was an accident","Yes, Rosa got hurt","Yes, it was physical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Ben mean to do it? Did it keep happening?","explain":"It was not on purpose, and it happened once. Getting hurt by accident is not bullying."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Raj told his teacher he is being bullied, but it has not stopped. What now?","choices":["Tell again, or tell another grown-up","Give up, telling does not work","Sort it out alone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Telling once is not always enough.","explain":"Keep telling until it stops. Raj can tell his teacher again, or tell a parent or another grown-up at school."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is bullying?","choices":["Ola calls Finn a mean name every day","Ola and Finn argue once about a game","Ola trips over Finn's bag by accident"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for something done on purpose, again and again.","explain":"Calling Finn a name every day is on purpose and repeated. That is verbal bullying. An argument once or an accident is not bullying."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Max hid Ava's bag on {n} days this week. How many times did it happen?","answer":"n","hint":"Count one for each day he hid the bag.","explain":"It happened {n} times. Again and again, on purpose, is part of what makes it bullying.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Mean messages about Jade keep coming in a group chat. What kind of bullying is it?","choices":["Cyberbullying","Physical bullying","It is not bullying"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where are the messages being sent?","explain":"Hurtful messages sent again and again online are cyberbullying. Jade should save them and show a trusted grown-up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} children see Sam being pushed. {k} of them go and get a teacher. How many are only watching?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take away the children who went to get help.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}. Those {=n-k} are bystanders. They could help too, by getting a grown-up or being kind to Sam.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Friends fall out sometimes. They shout, then say sorry and play again. That is not bullying. Bullying is different. Someone hurts you on purpose. They do it again and again. And it is hard for you to make them stop.","Why is it hard to stop? The bully may be older or bigger. They may have a gang of friends. They may know a secret. That is called power.","There are four kinds. Physical is pushing or breaking your things. Verbal is mean names or threats. Social is leaving you out or telling lies about you. Cyber is mean messages or posts online.","Bullying is never your fault. Tell a grown-up you trust. Save mean messages to show them. If it does not stop, tell again. If you see it, be an upstander. Do not laugh along. Be kind to the person, and get a grown-up."],"adult":["In England, Relationships Education is taught in every primary school. It covers the different types of bullying, including cyberbullying, and the harm it does. It also covers what bystanders should do (mainly tell an adult) and how to get help. The Anti-Bullying Alliance defines bullying as the repeated, intentional hurting of one person or group by another, where there is an imbalance of power. StopBullying.gov in the US uses the same three parts: unwanted aggressive behaviour, repetition, and a real or perceived power imbalance.","Key terms for this age: repeated, on purpose (intentional), power imbalance, physical, verbal, social or relational bullying (leaving out, rumours), cyberbullying, bystander, upstander, trusted adult and report. Many schools teach the STOP check: Several Times On Purpose. Power can come from size or age, from numbers, from being popular, or from knowing something private.","Common mistakes: calling every argument bullying, so the word loses its weight. Or the opposite: deciding that name-calling or leaving someone out is not real bullying because nobody was hit. Children often think telling makes it worse. They may hit back, reply to cruel messages, or delete them so there is no proof. A common adult slip is to say \"just ignore it\" or \"sort it out yourselves\" when the child faces a power gap they cannot fix alone.","In the lab your child marks the days a story happened on a week strip, to tell a falling out from a pattern. They sort accidents from things done on purpose. They load a see-saw with the bully's power, then level it with helpers. They sort examples into the four kinds, and sort actions into ones that help and ones that make it worse."]},"familyTip":"Ask, \"What would you do if someone kept being mean to a friend at school?\" and agree together who their trusted grown-ups are, at school and at home. If your child tells you about bullying, listen calmly, thank them for telling, write down what happened and when, and speak to the school.","keyIdea":"Bullying is hurting someone on purpose, again and again, when it is hard for them to stop it because the other person or group has more power. It can be physical (hitting, breaking things), verbal (names, threats), social (leaving someone out, spreading rumours) or cyberbullying (online). A one-off falling out or an accident is not bullying. Bullying is never the fault of the person it happens to. If it happens to you or you see it, tell a trusted grown-up, save any messages, and keep telling until it stops.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Bullying or a falling out? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Bullying","Not bullying"],"items":[["Kit hides Mo's lunch every day and laughs",0],["Two friends argue once, then make up",1],["A gang tells Ana she can't play, all week",0],["Ben bumps Jo by accident in the line",1],["Mean posts about Leo, again and again",0],["Sam and Ella both want the swing, once",1],["An older boy takes Raj's ball every playtime",0],["A friend says sorry after a game gets rough",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbully","caption":"Flagship lab: falling out or bullying on a week strip, on purpose or accident, a power see-saw, the four kinds, and what helps.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_yCmYV9ruQu":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Ivy films Zak being mean to Mia and shows her friends. What is Ivy doing?","choices":["Joining in","Being an upstander","Nothing wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does filming help Mia, or give Zak an audience?","explain":"Filming and sharing it gives Zak an audience and hurts Mia more. That is joining in."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which of these is a safe upstander move?","choices":["Ask the child to come and play with you","Shout mean words back","Hit the unkind child"],"answer":0,"hint":"A safe move helps the hurt child without making it bigger.","explain":"Standing with the child helps them and keeps you safe. Being mean back or hitting makes it worse."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why can a big crowd mean that nobody helps?","choices":["Each one waits for someone else","Big crowds are always kind","Nobody in a crowd can see"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what each child in the crowd is thinking.","explain":"Each one thinks someone else will help, so nobody does. Be the first, and others will join."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{n} children watch someone being teased. {k} of them laugh. How many are only watching, not laughing?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the laughing children away from all the children watching.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k} bystanders. They could still choose to help.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia looked sad at lunch, but it is over now. What can an upstander do later?","choices":["Ask Mia if she is OK","Forget about it","Tell everyone what happened"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can you show Mia that someone cares?","explain":"Checking in later is an upstander move. It shows Mia she is not on her own."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You tell a teacher that a child is bullied every day. This is…","choices":["Keeping the child safe","Telling tales","Being mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Telling tales is to get someone into trouble. Why are you telling?","explain":"You are telling to keep a child safe, so it is the right thing to do, not telling tales."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Raj sees Zak being unkind and walks past without a word. What is Raj?","choices":["A bystander","An upstander","The one being unkind"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Raj help, join in, or do nothing?","explain":"Raj saw it and did nothing, so Raj is a bystander. He could choose a safe way to help."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Kai stands with Mia. Then {k} friends come and stand with them. How many children stand with Mia now, not counting Mia?","answer":"k+1","hint":"Count Kai, then add the friends who came.","explain":"Kai plus {k} friends is {=k+1} children standing with Mia. A group makes the unkind child stop.","params":{"k":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Bystanders and upstanders: Kai sees Zak take Mia's ball. Kai says, 'Give it back, that is not kind.' What is Kai?","choices":["An upstander","A bystander","Joining in"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Kai help Mia, watch, or join in with Zak?","explain":"Kai spoke up to help Mia in a safe way, so Kai is an upstander."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} children watch Zak tease Mia, and they all laugh. Then {k} of them stop laughing and walk off with Mia. How many are still laughing?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the children who stopped away from all the children who laughed.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}. Each child who stops laughing makes Zak's audience smaller.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Two children are pushing hard and someone could get hurt. What should an upstander do?","choices":["Get a grown-up fast","Push back to stop them","Watch to see what happens"],"answer":0,"hint":"When someone could get hurt, what keeps everyone safe?","explain":"Pushing back could get you hurt, and watching lets it go on. Get a grown-up fast."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ada is the first to help a child who is left out. Then {k} more children join her. How many helpers are there now?","answer":"k+1","hint":"Count Ada, then count on the children who joined her.","explain":"Ada plus {k} more makes {=k+1} helpers. Once one child goes first, others follow.","params":{"k":[1,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Zak is mean to Mia. Other kids see it. Each one has a choice. A kid who joins in laughs or cheers. A bystander just looks on. An upstander helps Mia.","Doing nothing is a choice too. If kids laugh, Zak has a crowd. If no one laughs, his show is over. So do not laugh. Walk off with Mia.","Lots of kids may see it. Each one thinks: someone else will help. Then no one does. Be the first one. Then the rest will join you.","Help in a safe way. Say: Stop, that is not kind. Ask Mia to play. If a kid could get hurt, get a grown-up fast. To tell to keep a kid safe is not to tell tales."],"adult":["This topic covers the bystander role in relationships education. In England, the statutory guidance for primary schools says pupils should learn about the responsibilities of bystanders, mainly reporting bullying to an adult, and how to get help. The IB PYP personal, social and physical education strand asks children to show care and stand up for others. At 7 to 9 the aim is to name the roles, choose a safe action and feel able to use it.","Key terms: a passive bystander watches and does nothing. Children who laugh, cheer or film are joining in, because they give the unkind child an audience. An upstander speaks up, stands with the target, tells an adult or checks in later. In Canadian playground studies, peers were there in most bullying episodes. When one peer stepped in, the bullying often stopped within seconds. The bystander effect, or diffusion of responsibility, is why a big crowd can mean less help: each person waits for someone else.","Common mistakes: thinking that doing nothing is neutral; thinking that laughing along is harmless; teaching children to step in with their hands, or to be mean back, which puts them at risk; and calling reporting telling tales. Be careful with the rule. Telling to keep someone safe is always right. Telling tales is telling to get someone into trouble. Safety comes first, so when someone could get hurt, the job is to fetch an adult fast.","In the lab your child sorts what each child does, stops a laughing crowd and watches the audience bar fall, and goes first so the others follow. Then they pick a safe move for scenes of low and high risk, and sort telling from telling tales."]},"familyTip":"Agree a family upstander plan: three safe moves your child can use, such as 'Stop, that's not kind', 'Come and play with us' and telling an adult. Act them out with toys, then ask after school: did anyone need an upstander today?","keyIdea":"When someone is being unkind, every child who sees it makes a choice. Joining in means laughing, cheering or filming. A bystander watches and does nothing. An upstander helps in a safe way: says stop, stands with the hurt child, tells a grown-up or checks in later. If someone could get hurt, get a grown-up fast. Telling to keep someone safe is never telling tales.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Who is an upstander? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Upstander","Bystander","Joins in"],"items":[["Says: Stop, that is not kind",0],["Watches and says nothing",1],["Laughs along",2],["Tells a teacher",0],["Films it on a phone",2],["Looks away and walks past",1],["Asks the hurt child to play",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagupst","caption":"Flagship lab: sort what each child does, stop a laughing crowd, be the first helper, pick a safe move for the risk, and sort telling from telling tales.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zIzJGkaj0Q":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one is fine to share in an online game?","choices":["Your favourite animal","Your password","The name of your school"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one does not help anyone find you or get into your account?","explain":"Your favourite animal is fine to share. A password and a school name are private."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Who is a trusted adult?","choices":["A parent, carer or teacher you know","Anyone online who says they are a grown-up","A player who gives you free coins"],"answer":0,"hint":"You must know them in real life.","explain":"A trusted adult is a parent, carer or teacher you know in real life. Anyone online could be pretending."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Someone you have never met sends a friend request. Their profile says “Lily, 9”. What should you do?","choices":["Say no, and ask a grown-up if unsure","Accept, because she is 9","Send her your address"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you be sure who is really there?","explain":"Say no. Anyone can type any name, age or photo, so only accept friends you know in real life."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Someone keeps posting mean comments about a classmate. What is it called?","choices":["Cyberbullying","A joke","Teamwork"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bullying that happens online.","explain":"It is cyberbullying: being mean to someone again and again online. Do not join in, and tell a trusted adult."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is your digital footprint?","choices":["Everything you post and do online","A photo of your foot","Your password"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the trail of posts in the lab.","explain":"Your digital footprint is everything you post and do online. It can stay there for a long time."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A mean post is shared by {n} children. Each of them shows {k} more. How many more children see it?","answer":"n*k","hint":"{n} children each show {k} more. Multiply.","explain":"{n} times {k} is {=n*k} more children, so sharing spreads the hurt. Do not share mean posts.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia has {n} online friends. She knows {r} of them in real life. How many are people she has never met?","answer":"n-r","hint":"Take the real-life friends away from all of them.","explain":"{n} take away {r} is {=n-r} people Mia has never met. She should ask a grown-up about those.","params":{"n":[8,15],"r":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You get a message that makes you feel worried. What is the first thing to do?","choices":["Stop and do not reply","Reply with something mean","Forward it to all your friends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Stop, block and report, tell.","explain":"Stop and do not reply first. Then block and report, and tell a trusted adult."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"You delete a post. Is it gone for good?","choices":["Not always: someone may have saved or shared it","Yes, always","Yes, if it was a photo"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could anyone have copied it first?","explain":"Not always. Someone may have saved, screenshotted or shared it before you deleted it, so think before you post."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one should you keep private online?","choices":["Your home address","Your favourite colour","That you like cats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could help a stranger find you?","explain":"Your home address is private, because it tells a stranger where you live. A favourite colour is fine to share."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You post a photo. {n} friends each share it with {k} other people. How many other people get it?","answer":"n*k","hint":"{n} friends, and each shares with {k} people. Multiply.","explain":"{n} friends times {k} people each is {=n*k} people, and you do not know most of them. Posts spread.","params":{"n":[2,6],"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A message says, “Send me a photo. Do not tell your mum.” What should you do?","choices":["Do not reply, block, and tell a trusted adult","Send the photo","Keep the secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of stop, block and report, tell.","explain":"Do not reply, block and report, and tell a trusted adult. A safe person never asks you to keep secrets from your family."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Kai's password has {a} letters and {b} numbers. How many characters must Kai keep secret?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the letters and the numbers.","explain":"{a} letters and {b} numbers make {=a+b} characters, and Kai keeps all of them secret.","params":{"a":[4,8],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why should you be kind online?","choices":["A real person with feelings reads it","Nobody reads online comments","Mean words online do not count"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who is behind the screen?","explain":"A real person with feelings reads every comment, so mean words online hurt just like spoken ones."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some facts about you are private. Your password, your home address, your school and your phone number are private. They could help a stranger find you. Your favourite colour is fine to share.","A real person reads every message. Mean words on a screen hurt just like mean words said out loud. Before you post, ask: would I say this to their face?","Sometimes a message feels wrong. It might be mean, or ask for a photo, or say “Keep it secret.” Stop. Do not reply. Block and report. Then tell a trusted adult, like a parent or a teacher. You will not be in trouble.","People online may not be who they say. Anyone can type any name or age. Only accept friends you know in real life. What you post can stay online, even if you delete it. So think before you post."],"adult":["This lesson starts digital citizenship: staying safe, kind and responsible online. It matches the IB PYP personal, social and physical education outcome on interactions. In England it covers the primary relationships education topics online relationships and internet safety and harms. The computing curriculum also asks children to use technology safely, respectfully and responsibly.","Key terms for ages 7 to 9: personal (private) information, password, username, trusted adult, cyberbullying, block, report, friend request, stranger and digital footprint. A trusted adult is a grown-up the child knows in real life, such as a parent, carer or teacher.","Common mistakes: thinking some details are harmless, such as a school name or a photo in uniform. Believing a mean comment or a laughing emoji does not hurt because it is only on a screen. Keeping a secret that an online contact asks for, or not telling for fear of losing the tablet. Trusting the name, age or photo on a profile. Thinking deleted posts are gone.","In the lab your child sorts facts into private or OK to share. They pick kind replies and see the real child behind each post. They choose the safe moves when a message asks for a photo. Then they decide on three friend requests and four posts, which build a digital footprint. Every drag or tap has a button twin."]},"familyTip":"Agree a family rule together: if anything online feels wrong, stop, show a grown-up, and nobody is in trouble for telling. Then look at one app your child uses and check who can see their posts.","keyIdea":"Being a good digital citizen means staying safe and being kind online. Keep personal information private: your password, address, school and phone number. Behind every screen is a real person with real feelings. If something online feels wrong: stop, block and report, and tell a trusted adult. Think before you post, because posts can stay online.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Private or OK to share? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🔒 Keep private","✅ OK to share"],"items":[["Your password",0],["Your home address",0],["Your phone number",0],["Your favourite colour",1],["That you like football",1],["A drawing of a dragon",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnetsafe","caption":"Flagship lab: sort private facts, reply kindly to real people, choose safe moves, check friend requests and think before you post.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_rkrG2w7WXI":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What would happen in a game of tag with no rules?","choices":["Nobody would know who is out","It would be more fun for all","Nothing would change"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about who is it and who is out.","explain":"With no rules, nobody would know who is out, so the game would not work. Rules help us play together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is a fair agreement for choosing a game?","choices":["Take turns to choose","The oldest always picks","Whoever shouts first picks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair means it works for everyone.","explain":"Taking turns to choose is fair, because everyone gets a go. The other two only work for one child."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why should you use a quiet voice in the library?","choices":["People are reading and thinking","Libraries are scary","Books do not like noise"],"answer":0,"hint":"What are people doing in a library?","explain":"Use a quiet voice because people in the library are reading and thinking. The rule fits what happens there."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mum says one biscuit each, then goes upstairs. What do you do?","choices":["Take one, so all get one","Take lots: she will not know","Hide some for later"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the rule is for: is it about being fair?","explain":"Take one, so everyone gets one. The rule is there to be fair, even when Mum cannot see."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Running is fine in the park. Is it fine at the pool?","choices":["No, the pool floor is wet","Yes, rules are the same everywhere","Yes, if you say sorry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the floor at a pool. Is it the same as grass?","explain":"No, because the pool floor is wet and slippery. Different places need different rules."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Who makes a class agreement?","choices":["The class, together","Only the loudest child","Nobody, it just happens"],"answer":0,"hint":"An agreement is made by a group.","explain":"The class makes it together. That is what makes it an agreement, and why it works for everyone."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} friends make an agreement. All of them are happy but 1. How many are happy?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take away the 1 sad friend.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. One friend is still sad, so try a fairer agreement.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why do we have a rule to hold hands crossing the road?","choices":["To keep us safe from cars","To make it fair","So we can win"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the cars on the road and what could happen.","explain":"Holding hands keeps us safe from cars. It is a safe rule."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A fair rule: each of the {n} children gets 2 stickers. How many stickers is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count 2 stickers for each child, then add them up.","explain":"{n} children with 2 stickers each is {=2*n} stickers. Everyone gets the same, so it is fair.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why do we walk, not run, inside?","choices":["So nobody trips and gets hurt","Because running is too much fun","So we are late"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what could happen if you ran.","explain":"We walk inside so nobody trips and gets hurt. That rule keeps us safe."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} children want a go on the slide. Each child gets 1 go. How many goes in all?","answer":"n","hint":"Count 1 go for each child.","explain":"{n} children with 1 go each make {=n} goes. Taking turns means every child gets a go.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The teacher goes out of the room. Do the class rules still count?","choices":["Yes, the reason is still there","No, nobody is watching","Only for the big children"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the floor still slippery when she goes?","explain":"Yes, the rules still count. The reason for a rule does not go away when nobody is looking."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A class has {n} rules. 1 rule is about being quiet. How many other rules are there?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the quiet rule away.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. Every one of those rules has a reason too.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A rule tells us what to do. Every rule has a reason. Walk inside, so you do not trip. Take turns on the swing, so all get a go.","Think of a game of tag with no rules. Who is it? Who is out? Nobody knows! Rules make the game work. They help us play together.","An agreement is a rule we make together. Your class can agree: one talks, the rest listen. A good agreement makes every friend happy.","Each place has its own rules. Be quiet in the library, so people can read. Walk at the pool, as the floor is wet. Keep a rule even when no grown-up can see. The reason is still there."],"adult":["At this age children are learning that rules are not just orders from adults. Rules exist for reasons: to keep people safe, to make things fair, and to help a group work together. The PSHE Association programme of study asks Key Stage 1 pupils to learn what rules are, why they are needed, and why different rules are needed for different situations. The non-statutory KS1 citizenship framework asks them to agree and follow rules for their group and classroom, and to understand how rules help them.","A common mistake is to explain rules only as 'because I said so'. Children then tend to keep rules only while an adult is watching. When you name the reason, such as 'walk, because the floor is slippery', the rule makes sense on its own, and children are more likely to keep it when you step away. Asking 'what could happen if we had no rule here?' helps them work out the reason for themselves.","An agreement is a rule a group makes together, like a class charter. Children this age can judge a fair agreement by asking whether it works for everyone, not just the loudest or the oldest child. Another mistake is to think fair means 'I get my way'. Voting, taking turns to choose and listening while one person talks are agreements they can try at home with siblings or at a playdate.","In the lab your child sorts rules by their reason: safe, fair or together. They watch a slide, a hall and a game of tag fall into chaos with no rule, then drag in the rule that fixes each one. A grown-up looks away and they choose whether the rule still counts. They pick group agreements and see every face react, then match rules to the library, the pool and football. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Make one family agreement together this week, such as how screen time or bath time works. Let everyone say what they think, then write it down and stick it up. When someone forgets, ask 'what was our reason?' instead of just repeating the rule.","keyIdea":"Rules are there for a reason. Some rules keep us safe. Some keep things fair. Some help us work together. With no rules, people get hurt, left out or muddled. An agreement is a rule a group makes together, and a good one works for everyone. Different places need different rules. We keep a rule because of its reason, so it still counts when nobody is watching.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Why is this rule here? Drag each rule, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Keeps us safe","Keeps it fair","Helps us work together"],"items":[["Hold hands to cross the road",0],["Wear a helmet on your bike",0],["Wait your turn to speak",1],["Share the new toys",1],["Everyone helps tidy up",2],["Agree on a plan for the den",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwhyrule","caption":"Flagship lab: sort rules by reason, fix places with no rules, keep a rule when nobody looks, make fair agreements and match rules to places.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_h_shhH-6DC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does UNCRC stand for?","choices":["UN Convention on the Rights of the Child","United Nations Children's Rules Club","UN Council for Rich Countries"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a promise about the rights of everyone under 18.","explain":"The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child has 54 articles; 42 of them set out the rights of everyone under 18."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Can a child lose their right to be safe for breaking a rule?","choices":["No, rights cannot be taken away","Yes, until they say sorry","Yes, if the rule was important"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are rights earned by good behaviour?","explain":"Rights are not earned, so they cannot be lost as a punishment. A privilege can be."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You have the right to say what you think. What is your responsibility?","choices":["Let others say what they think too","Talk over people who disagree","Only speak to friends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Everyone has the same right as you.","explain":"Because others have the same right, respecting it means listening and taking turns."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Who has the main duty to provide free schools?","choices":["The government","Children","Shops"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who builds schools and pays teachers?","explain":"Governments are duty bearers: they must make the right to education happen (Article 28)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Two friends want the same swing. What is a fair fix?","choices":["Take turns on it","The stronger one keeps it","Nobody uses it"],"answer":0,"hint":"A fair fix keeps both children's right to play.","explain":"Taking turns keeps the right to play for both. Grabbing or banning it takes it from someone."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mo posts a photo of Lily without asking. Which right is affected?","choices":["Lily's right to privacy","Mo's right to play","Lily's right to food"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lily did not agree to the photo being shared.","explain":"Article 16 gives every child a right to privacy, so ask before sharing photos."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A school takes away {a} minutes of golden time from a child. How many of the child's rights does that remove?","answer":"0","hint":"Is golden time a right or a privilege?","explain":"The answer is 0. Golden time is a privilege, so removing {a} minutes is allowed and the child keeps every right.","params":{"a":[5,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A charter lists {r} rights. The class writes one way to respect each right. How many ways do they write?","answer":"r","hint":"One way for each right on the list.","explain":"{r} rights need {r} ways to respect them, one for each.","params":{"r":[3,8]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a right for every child?","choices":["Clean water and healthy food","A new bike","A holiday abroad"],"answer":0,"hint":"A right is something every child needs to live, grow and be safe.","explain":"Clean water and healthy food are rights (Article 24). A bike and a holiday are nice wants."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A class sorts {a} cards. {b} of them are rights and the rest are wants. How many cards are wants?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Every card is either a right or a want. Take the rights away from the total.","explain":"{a} cards minus {b} rights leaves {=a-b} wants.","params":{"a":[8,12],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Leo hit someone. Which consequence is allowed?","choices":["Losing his golden time","Going without lunch","Being called names by the teacher"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is a privilege, and which ones are rights?","explain":"Golden time is a privilege, so it can be taken away. Food and dignity are rights, so they cannot."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a class of {n} children, every child has the right to be heard. How many children have that right?","answer":"n","hint":"Do any children have to earn the right first?","explain":"All {n} children have it. Rights are universal: every child has them, with no conditions.","params":{"n":[20,32]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A right is something every child should have. You need it to live, grow, learn and be safe. Going to school is a right. So is being kept safe from harm.","A want is different. A new phone is nice, but you can live and grow without it. Rights are about needs, not wants.","Rights belong to every child. You do not earn them by being good, and you do not lose them by being naughty. A treat like golden time can be taken away. Food and safety cannot.","Everyone has the same rights as you. So your part is to respect theirs. If you have the right to be heard, you listen when others speak. Grown-ups and the government have a job too: they must make rights happen."],"adult":["In England, the PSHE Association programme for Key Stage 2 asks pupils to learn that human rights are there to protect everyone, and how rights relate to responsibilities. The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child was adopted in 1989. The UK ratified it in 1991. It has 54 articles, 42 of which give rights to everyone under 18.","Key words: a right holder is the child. A duty bearer is an adult or body that must make the right happen, such as the government, teachers and other adults working for the state, and parents and carers. Rights are universal and inherent: they cannot be earned, traded or removed. A privilege, such as extra screen time, can be given or withdrawn. Article 12 gives the right to be heard. Article 19 is protection from harm. Article 28 is education, and school discipline must respect the child's dignity.","Common mistakes: saying children must earn their rights, or lose them for bad behaviour. Confusing wants with rights. Thinking a right means always getting your own way. Thinking only children have responsibilities, when adults and governments are the main duty bearers. Be careful with the phrase no rights without responsibilities. UNICEF UK's Rights Respecting Schools guidance warns against it, because rights are unconditional.","In the lab, learners sort rights from wants and match a right to the respect it asks of them. They find which punishments would break a child's rights, settle three everyday clashes on a balance with a fair fix, and hand each right to its duty bearer."]},"familyTip":"When a family rule is broken, talk about what is a privilege (screen time, a treat) and what is a right (food, safety, being listened to). Ask: which ones could we take away, and which ones never?","keyIdea":"A right is something every child should have so they can live, grow, learn, be safe and be heard. The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child (UNCRC) has 54 articles, and 42 of them set out children's rights, such as the right to education and the right to be safe from harm. Rights belong to every child and are not earned by good behaviour, so they cannot be taken away as a punishment; privileges can. Because everyone has the same rights, each of us has a responsibility to respect other people's rights, and adults and governments have a duty to make rights happen.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Right or privilege? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Right (never taken away)","Privilege (can be lost)"],"items":[["Being safe from harm",0],["Extra screen time",1],["Going to school",0],["Choosing the film tonight",1],["Being listened to",0],["Sweets after dinner",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrights","caption":"Flagship lab: sort rights from wants, mirror a right onto everyone else, find punishments that break rights, balance clashes, and match rights to duty bearers.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_6xj94tmpi-":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is a dilemma hard to decide?","choices":["Both choices protect something good","One choice is clearly bad","There are no consequences"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what each side cares about.","explain":"In a dilemma both sides protect a value, so whatever you choose costs something you care about."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A friend makes you promise not to tell that someone is hurting him. What should you do?","choices":["Tell a trusted adult","Keep the promise","Tell other children"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which matters more here: the promise or his safety?","explain":"A secret that hides harm should go to a trusted adult. Safety comes before the promise."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which consequence should weigh the most?","choices":["Someone could be badly hurt","Someone may be cross for a day","Someone may feel embarrassed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which harm cannot be undone?","explain":"Being badly hurt may not be undone. Feeling cross or embarrassed passes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is NOT a real reason that a choice is right?","choices":["Everyone else is doing it","It keeps someone safe","It is fair to the whole group"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the reason name a value or who is helped?","explain":"“Everyone else is doing it” names no value and no one who is helped, so it is not a real reason."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You are picking a team. Which choice respects both loyalty and fairness?","choices":["Pick by effort and skill, and kindly tell your friend why","Always pick your best friend first","Stop talking to your friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the choice that is fair to everyone and still kind to your friend.","explain":"Picking by effort is fair, and explaining kindly keeps the friendship. Both values are respected."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"The tell side of a balance holds weights {a} and {b}. The keep side holds weights 2 and {c}. How heavy is the tell side?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add only the weights on the tell side.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so the tell side weighs {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[1,3],"c":[1,2]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} children vote on a hard choice. {a} of them vote to tell a teacher. How many vote not to tell?","answer":"n-a","hint":"Take the tell votes away from all the children.","explain":"{n} − {a} = {=n-a} children vote not to tell.","params":{"n":[5,8],"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which values clash when you see a friend cheat in a test?","choices":["Loyalty and fairness","Kindness and courage","Safety and honesty"],"answer":0,"hint":"One value is about your friend. The other is about the rest of the class.","explain":"Sticking by your friend is loyalty, and the class being treated equally is fairness."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What is a “third way” in a dilemma?","choices":["A choice that respects both values","Doing nothing at all","Asking a friend to choose for you"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a choice that keeps both values in view.","explain":"A third way finds a choice that respects both values, like being honest in a kind way."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Your friend shows you a drawing she is proud of. You think it is not very good. Which two values pull against each other?","choices":["Honesty and kindness","Safety and fairness","Courage and loyalty"],"answer":0,"hint":"One value is about telling the truth. The other is about not hurting her feelings.","explain":"Telling the truth is honesty, and not hurting her is kindness. You can do both: say which part you like and give one gentle tip."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"On a balance, the reasons to tell weigh {a} and {b}. The reasons to keep a secret weigh {c}. How much heavier is the tell side?","answer":"a+b-c","hint":"Add the two weights on the tell side, then take away the keep side.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} on the tell side, and {=a+b} − {c} = {=a+b-c}. The tell side is heavier by {=a+b-c}.","params":{"a":[3,5],"b":[2,3],"c":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is a real reason to give back money you found?","choices":["It belongs to someone who may need it","Someone might have seen you","Your friends would do it"],"answer":0,"hint":"A real reason names who is helped or harmed, or a value.","explain":"The owner may need the money, so returning it is honest and fair. Being seen or copying friends is not a reason it is right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A dilemma has {a} possible choices. You rule out {b} because they hurt someone. How many choices are left to weigh?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Start with all the choices and take away the ones you ruled out.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} choices are left to weigh.","params":{"a":[5,7],"b":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some choices are easy: do not take your friend's lunch. A dilemma is harder. Every option costs something you care about. Gran knits you a jumper you do not like and asks if you love it. Honesty says tell the truth. Kindness says do not hurt her. Both are good, and they pull different ways.","Start by naming the values. Values are the things people care about: honesty, kindness, loyalty, safety, fairness and courage. When you name the two that clash, you can see why the choice feels hard. It does not mean one side is bad.","Next, weigh the consequences. Ask who could be helped or harmed by each choice, and how badly. Ben makes you promise not to tell that he will cycle to the lake alone after dark. Breaking the promise may make him cross for a while. Keeping it could let him get badly hurt, and that cannot be undone. Safety wins, so tell a trusted adult.","Check your reasons. Good reasons name a value or a person who is helped or harmed. “Everyone does it”, “I won't get caught” and “because I said so” are not reasons something is right. Last, look for a third way. You can thank Gran for her time and say which part you like. That is honest and kind at once."],"adult":["The PSHE Association plan for Key Stage 2 asks pupils to manage dilemmas and to see that values can clash. It also asks them to know when to break a confidence. A secret that puts someone at risk of harm should go to a trusted adult. CASEL calls this skill responsible decision-making: weighing the effects on self and others, with care for ethics and safety.","A simple routine for nine to eleven year olds has four moves. Name the two values in tension. Weigh the consequences: who is affected, how badly, and can the harm be undone? Test the reasons. Popularity, a rule with no reason, or the chance of being caught do not make a choice right. Then look for a third option that keeps both values, such as honesty said kindly.","Common mistakes: treating every dilemma as good against bad, so one side is written off as wrong. Keeping a promise when the secret hides danger. Counting how many reasons sit on each side, not how serious they are. Accepting that everyone does it. Seeing only the two extremes. Be careful to praise the thinking, not just the answer, since fair people can disagree on close cases.","In the lab your child names the two values in three dilemmas. They put five consequence cards on a balance and watch harm that cannot be undone tip it towards telling. Then they sort real reasons from fake ones and pick the option that respects both values."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, share a small dilemma from your own day, such as a friend asking you to cover for them. Ask your child which two values clash, who could be hurt and how badly, and whether there is a third way. Talk about the reasons rather than judging the answer.","keyIdea":"An ethical dilemma is a choice where two good values pull different ways, such as honesty and kindness, or loyalty and safety. Name the values first. Then weigh the consequences: who could be helped or harmed, and how badly. Harm that cannot be undone weighs the most, so a secret that hides danger should go to a trusted adult. Give real reasons, not “everyone does it”, and look for a choice that respects both values.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a good reason for a choice? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Good reason","Not a real reason"],"items":[["Someone could get hurt",0],["Everyone else does it",1],["It is fair to everyone",0],["I won't get caught",1],["It would hurt their feelings",0],["Because I said so",1],["It keeps a promise that is safe to keep",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdilem","caption":"Flagship lab: name the two values in a dilemma, weigh consequence cards on a balance, sort real reasons, and find a choice that respects both values.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_RNeEF1JU4J":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"In a school of {a} pupils, {b} pupils need a phone for a medical reason. How many pupils would a full ban with no exceptions affect without any problem?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the pupils with a medical need away from the total.","explain":"{a} - {b} = {=a-b} pupils. The other {b} would be put at risk, which is why fair rules can have exceptions.","params":{"a":[200,400],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"{n} pupils say a phone ban is right because everyone they know agrees. What kind of reason is that?","choices":["A popularity reason, which is weak on its own","The strongest kind of reason","A fact that settles it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it say who is helped and how?","explain":"Lots of people agreeing does not explain who is helped or harmed, so on its own it is a weak, popularity reason.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which of these is a values question?","choices":["Should phones be allowed at break time?","How many pupils own a phone?","What time does school finish?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for words like should, fair or right.","explain":"Should phones be allowed asks what is right, so people argue it with reasons. The others can be checked."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is a strawman?","choices":["A weak, twisted version of someone's view that is easy to knock down","The best version of someone's view","A fact that proves you right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the unfair summaries in the lab.","explain":"A strawman twists the other side into something silly. A fair thinker sums up their best reason instead."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is the strongest reason for a phone ban?","choices":["Fewer phones buzzing means fewer distractions in lessons","My friends all think so","Phones are annoying"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reason says who it helps and how?","explain":"Fewer distractions explains who is helped and how, so it is stronger than a feeling or a popular view."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Who is affected by a school phone rule?","choices":["Pupils, parents and teachers","Only pupils","Only the head teacher"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the four people in the lab.","explain":"A phone rule affects pupils, parents who want to reach them and teachers who run lessons, so a fair rule thinks about all of them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"One study found test scores went up after phone bans. What is the best way to use this?","choices":["As one piece of evidence, alongside other studies","As proof that ends the argument","Ignore it because it is only numbers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is one study the last word?","explain":"One study is useful evidence, but other studies found different things, so weigh it with the rest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Before you argue against someone's view, what should you be able to do?","choices":["Say their view in a way they would call fair","Prove they are silly","Talk louder than them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would they agree with your summary?","explain":"If you can say their view fairly, you understand it, and your own argument gets stronger."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In a survey, {n} out of 30 pupils in a class say phones distract them. Does that number settle whether a ban is fair?","choices":["No, it is useful evidence, but fairness also needs reasons about values","Yes, numbers always settle it","Yes, if the number is big enough"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is fair a word you can count, or a word you argue about?","explain":"The survey is a fact that helps the argument, but fair is a values question, so it also needs reasons about who is helped and who loses out.","params":{"n":[8,22]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A class lists {a} reasons for a ban and {b} reasons against it. How many reasons is that in total to weigh?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the reasons on both sides.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} reasons. The number is only a start: one strong reason can matter more than several weak ones.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is the fairest summary of people who are against a phone ban?","choices":["Some families want children to have a phone for the way home","They only care about games","They have no reasons at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would people on that side agree with your summary?","explain":"Families wanting a phone for the way home is a real reason that supporters would accept, so it is a fair summary, not a strawman."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Zara was for a ban. Then she read a study showing a ban did not help wellbeing, so she changed her view a little. What does this show?","choices":["Good thinking: she let new evidence move her","Weakness: she should never change her mind","She was wrong to read the study"],"answer":0,"hint":"What should a good thinker do with strong new evidence?","explain":"Weighing new evidence and adjusting your view is good thinking. Holding on no matter what is not."}],"explain":{"kid":["Should your school ban phones? In England, the government told schools in 2024 that they should stop phones being used during the school day. Some schools agree. Some people do not. This is a real issue, so it is a good one to practise on.","First, split the questions. How many pupils bring a phone is a fact. You can count it. Is a ban fair is a values question. People can know the same facts and still disagree, so they need reasons.","Next, ask who is affected. A parent may want to reach their child after school. A teacher may want quiet lessons. A pupil with diabetes may need a phone app to check their blood sugar. A good rule thinks about all of them.","Then take a side and give a reason that says who it helps and how. Say the other side's best reason fairly, not a silly version. If strong new evidence turns up, it is fine to change your mind. That is good thinking, not losing."],"adult":["This fits Key Stage 2 PSHE work on rules, rights and duties. Pupils talk about issues in the news and learn that people hold different views. It also matches the IB Primary Years aim of forming a view and backing it with reasons.","The example is real. In 2024 the Department for Education told schools in England to stop phone use in the school day. It is advice, not law, and it allows exceptions such as medical needs. The evidence is mixed. A 2015 study in four English cities found test scores rose after bans, most for pupils who were behind. A 2025 study of 30 schools found no difference in how well pupils felt.","Common mistakes: treating a values question as if one fact could settle it; giving a popular reason, such as everyone thinks so; attacking a weak version of the other side, called a strawman; and seeing a change of mind as weakness. Ask your child to say the other view in words its supporters would accept.","In the lab your child sorts fact and values questions and matches reasons to a pupil, a parent, a teacher and a pupil with diabetes. They place a marker on a line from against to for and back it with a reason. Then they pick the fair summary of each side and sort new evidence."]},"familyTip":"Pick a rule from the news or from home, such as screen time at weekends. Each take a side and give one reason about who it helps. Then swap: each of you sums up the other side so fairly that the other person says, yes, that is what I mean. Finish by asking what new fact could change your mind.","keyIdea":"A real-world issue, like whether schools should ban phones, mixes fact questions you can check with values questions you argue with reasons. Think it through by asking who is affected, taking a position with a reason, summing up the other side fairly, and changing your mind if strong new evidence comes in.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can you check it, or do people argue it with reasons? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fact question","Values question"],"items":[["How many cars pass the school each morning?",0],["Should cars be banned outside the gate?",1],["How long is lunch break?",0],["Is it fair that older pupils go first at lunch?",1],["What does the school rule say about uniform?",0],["Should pupils choose their own uniform?",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstance","caption":"Flagship lab: on the school phone ban, split fact from values, match reasons to people, take a side, be fair and sort evidence.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_JiZ3H90Xg8":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which choice is uncertain rather than risky?","choices":["whether you will enjoy a new hobby","rolling a 1 on a dice","tossing heads on a coin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could anyone tell you the odds in advance?","explain":"No one can give odds for enjoying a hobby, so it is uncertain. Dice and coins have known odds, so they are risks."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"In a survey of {n} drivers, {s} said they were safer than the middle driver. How many of them at least must be wrong?","answer":"s-n/2","hint":"Only half of {n} can be above the middle. How many more than that said yes?","explain":"Half of {n} is {=n/2}. {s} − {=n/2} = {=s-n/2}, so at least {=s-n/2} must be wrong. That gap is optimism bias.","params":{"n":[20,40],"s":[16,38]},"where":"n%2==0 && s>n/2 && s<=n"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Nobody in a group questions a dangerous plan, and the one person who doubts it is told to stop being boring. What is this?","choices":["groupthink","availability bias","uncertainty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the group checking the plan, or pushing everyone to agree?","explain":"Pressure on someone who disagrees is a classic sign of groupthink. Asking a real question or calling a vote breaks it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the first step of the decision plan?","choices":["pause","choose","ask the group to vote"],"answer":0,"hint":"Before options or facts, what slows the fast brain down?","explain":"Pausing gives you time to notice a bias before it decides for you. Then list options, check facts and odds, check values, choose, review."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Priya says, 'I know about optimism bias, so it can't affect me.' What is wrong?","choices":["knowing about a bias does not switch it off","optimism bias only affects adults","nothing, she is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do biases go away just because you can name them?","explain":"Everyone has these biases, even experts. Naming them helps only if you also use a check, like looking at the real numbers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Teenagers in a study took more risks in a driving game when what happened?","choices":["friends were watching","they were alone","an adult was in charge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the groupthink step.","explain":"Gardner and Steinberg (2005) found teens took more risks with peers watching, while adults barely changed. Knowing this helps you plan ahead."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A bag has {r} red and {b} blue counters. How many chances out of {=r+b} are there of picking red?","answer":"r","hint":"Each counter is one chance. How many are red?","explain":"{r} of the {=r+b} counters are red, so there are {r} chances in {=r+b}. Known odds make it a risk.","params":{"r":[1,6],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"The facts don't settle whether to join a new club. What should help you decide?","choices":["what you value, such as making friends or trying new things","whatever your friends are doing","whichever feels best right now"],"answer":0,"hint":"When the odds can't be known, what is left to guide you?","explain":"Under uncertainty, your values carry the decision. Following the group risks groupthink, and 'right now' risks present bias."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a risk, not an uncertainty?","choices":["drawing a red card from a full pack","whether a new friend will stay a friend","what music will be popular in ten years"],"answer":0,"hint":"For a risk, you can work out the odds exactly before it happens.","explain":"Half of a full pack is red, so the odds are 26 in 52, or 1 in 2. The other two have odds nobody can know."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A class of {n} people ranks how safe they are. At most how many can be safer than the middle person?","answer":"n/2","hint":"The middle splits the group in half. How many are on one side?","explain":"Half of {n} is {=n/2}, so at most {=n/2} people can be above the middle. If more claim it, some are showing optimism bias.","params":{"n":[10,40]},"where":"n%2==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A raffle has {t} tickets and you have {k}. Out of {t}, how many chances do you have to win? (This is a risk: the odds are known.)","answer":"k","hint":"Each ticket is one chance. Count your tickets.","explain":"You hold {k} of the {t} tickets, so you have {k} chances in {t}. Known odds make this a risk.","params":{"t":[50,200],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ben saw three news stories about lightning strikes and now won't play football in the rain. Which bias is most likely?","choices":["availability bias","present bias","groupthink"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did something vivid make the danger feel more common?","explain":"Vivid news made a rare event easy to picture, so it feels likely. That is availability. Checking the real numbers is the fix."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why do many people take £10 today over £11 tomorrow, but wait for £11 when both are a year away?","choices":["present bias: now feels bigger than later","optimism bias: they think they'll be richer","the extra £1 is worth more in a year"],"answer":0,"hint":"The wait is one day both times. What changed?","explain":"Only the distance from now changed. A reward today pulls harder than one tomorrow, but in a year both feel far away. That is present bias."}],"explain":{"kid":["A risk is a choice where you can know the odds. A fair dice lands on 6 one time in 6. Uncertainty is a choice where nobody knows the odds, like how a new school will feel. For uncertain choices, your values matter even more.","Your brain uses shortcuts to judge what is likely, and they can trick you. The availability bias makes things you can easily picture feel common. Shark attacks are in the news, but they kill fewer than 15 people a year. Malaria, spread by mosquitoes, killed about 600,000 people in 2022.","Present bias makes now feel bigger than later, so you put off revision. Optimism bias says 'it won't happen to me'. Groupthink is when a group wants to agree so much that nobody checks the plan. Teenagers take more risks when friends are watching.","A plan slows you down so the shortcuts have less pull: pause, list your options, check the facts and odds, check your values, choose, and then look back at how it went."],"adult":["Ages 11 to 12. This fits PSHE Association KS3 work on managing risk and making choices, and the CASEL skill of responsible decision-making. Key terms: risk and uncertainty (Knight, 1921), heuristic, the availability heuristic (Tversky and Kahneman, 1973), present bias, optimism bias (Weinstein, 1980), groupthink (Janis, 1972), and values.","The point is not that mental shortcuts are bad. They are fast and often good enough. They go wrong in four places: rare but vivid events, a reward now against a cost later, judging your own risk, and choices made in a group. In one study, teens took more risks in a driving game when friends watched, but adults hardly changed (Gardner and Steinberg, 2005).","Common mistakes: treating an uncertain choice as if the odds were known; thinking a bias means someone is stupid, when everyone has them; believing that knowing about a bias makes it go away; and thinking facts always give the answer. When the facts run out, a good thinker names what matters to them and decides on that.","In the lab the learner sorts six cases into risk or uncertainty. They watch three shark stories push up a 'feels likely' bar while the real numbers stay put. They make the £10 today or £11 tomorrow choice (Thaler, 1981), drag a line to split 30 drivers at the middle, flag groupthink in a group chat about jumping off a pier, and put a six-step plan in order."]},"familyTip":"Next time a news story scares someone at home, ask together: 'How often does this really happen?' and look it up. When a choice comes up (a trip, a phone deal, a party), try the plan out loud: what are the options, what are the facts, what matters to us? Then talk later about how it went.","keyIdea":"A risk is a choice where the odds can be known (1 in 6 for a dice); uncertainty is a choice where nobody can know the odds (whether you will like a new club). Our brains judge both with shortcuts that can mislead: the availability bias (vivid stories make rare things feel likely), present bias (now feels bigger than later), optimism bias ('it won't happen to me') and groupthink (a group agrees instead of checking). Teens are especially drawn to risk when friends are watching. A simple plan helps: pause, list options, check facts and odds, check your values, choose, then review.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which bias is pulling? Drag each thought, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["availability bias","present bias","optimism bias"],"items":[["I saw a shark film, so I won't swim in the sea.",0],["I'll save next month; I want the trainers today.",1],["Other people crash on bikes, but not me.",2],["After plane-crash news, flying feels deadly.",0],["One more episode, then I'll do homework.",1],["I won't get caught out by a scam text.",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrisk","caption":"Flagship lab: sort risk from uncertainty, watch news inflate a fear, choose now or later, split a class at the middle, flag groupthink, order a plan.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_WtO50EZQkf":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a filter bubble?","choices":["When an app shows you more and more of what you already like","A spam filter for email","A setting that blocks all news"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happened to the feed after four likes.","explain":"Recommendation apps show more of what you click, so you see fewer other views and topics."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why can very clever people believe false posts?","choices":["Seeing a claim many times and strong feelings make it feel true","Clever people never believe false posts","False posts are always easy to spot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does feeling sure mean you checked?","explain":"Repeats make claims feel familiar and true, and emotion cuts checking short. Everyone needs checking habits."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A post is shared by 1 person, and each person shares with {s} others for 3 rounds. How many people see it in round 3?","answer":"s*s*s","hint":"Multiply by {s} for each round: {s}, then {s} × {s}, then once more.","explain":"{s} × {s} × {s} = {=s*s*s} people in round 3 alone.","params":{"s":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A post has 80,000 shares. What does that tell you?","choices":["That it is popular, not that it is true","That it must be true","That experts checked it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do false posts get shared too?","explain":"Shares measure attention. False, shocking posts are often shared more than true ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ella posts a mean comment she would never say to someone's face. What is the problem?","choices":["Her online self should match her real self","Nothing, online does not count","Only that she used capitals"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the person reading it real?","explain":"The person reading it is real and so is Ella. Be the same person online as offline."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the best way to widen your feed?","choices":["Search for and follow new topics on purpose","Like only the posts you already agree with","Scroll faster"],"answer":0,"hint":"The app learns from what you choose.","explain":"Choosing new things teaches the app to show a wider mix."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Out of {n} posts in a feed, 3 are about something new to you. How many are more of what you already like?","answer":"n-3","hint":"Take the 3 new posts away from all {n}.","explain":"{n} − 3 = {=n-3} posts repeat what you already like.","params":{"n":[8,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which of these is reading sideways?","choices":["Searching the name of the site to see what others say","Reading every word of the post twice","Looking at the site's logo"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do you stay on the page or leave it?","explain":"Lateral reading means leaving the page and checking other sources about it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A shocking post is shared by 1 person. Each person who sees it shares it with {s} more. How many new people see it in round 2?","answer":"s*s","hint":"Round 1 reaches {s} people. Each of them shares with {s} more.","explain":"Round 2 is {s} × {s} = {=s*s} new people. Shares multiply each round, which is why shocking posts spread so fast.","params":{"s":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"You find a scary claim on a site you have never heard of. What do fact-checkers do first?","choices":["Open new tabs to see what others say about the site","Read the site's About page","Check how many likes it has"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a site be trusted to describe itself?","explain":"Fact-checkers leave the page and read sideways. A site's own About page only says what it wants you to believe."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Your feed shows 10 posts. {g} are about gaming. How many posts are about other things?","answer":"10-g","hint":"Take the gaming posts away from all 10.","explain":"10 − {g} = {=10-g} posts about anything else. The more you like one thing, the smaller that number gets.","params":{"g":[4,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which part of a post is a trick, not evidence?","choices":["SHARE before they delete this!","Published by the NHS on 3 May","A link to the full study"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part tries to make you act before you think?","explain":"Rushing you to share is a manipulation trick. A named source, a date and a link are things you can check."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You took a funny photo of three friends. What should you do before posting it?","choices":["Ask them if they are happy for it to go online","Nothing, you took it","Post it and delete it if anyone complains"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whose faces are in the photo?","explain":"Their faces are theirs. Ask first. Once it is online, others can copy it, so deleting later may be too late."}],"explain":{"kid":["Apps watch what you tap and like. Then they show you more of the same. Like four gaming posts and your feed fills up with games. You see less of everything else. This is called a filter bubble. Searching for new things on purpose helps you break out.","False posts often spread faster than true ones. A big study of Twitter found that false news was 70% more likely to be shared. Why? It is often new, shocking or scary. Strong feelings make us share before we think.","Clever people get fooled too. A claim we see again and again starts to feel true. So do not trust a gut feeling. Use a habit. Leave the page and open a new tab. Search for who runs the site and what others say about the claim. This is called reading sideways, or lateral reading.","Your online self is still you. If you would not say it to a face, do not post it. Photos and details of other people belong to them too. Ask before you post a photo of a friend. Never share someone's address or private chats."],"adult":["Ages 12 to 13. This fits the PSHE Association programme for Key Stage 3. It covers media literacy and digital resilience: how apps choose what we see, and how to judge a source. It also fits the statutory RSE and Health Education guidance on online and media. Key terms: digital footprint, recommendation algorithm, filter bubble, misinformation, disinformation, lateral reading, source evaluation, consent.","The evidence is strong. Vosoughi, Roy and Aral studied Twitter in 2018. False stories were 70% more likely to be retweeted than true ones, and spread faster. Novelty and emotion drove it. The Stanford History Education Group found that fact-checkers judge an unknown site by leaving it. They read what others say about it. Students and even professors stayed on the page. Seeing a claim again and again also makes it feel truer. This is the illusory truth effect.","Common mistakes: trusting a site's own About page; treating likes or shares as evidence; and thinking only foolish people are misled, which makes people overconfident. Children may also think a feed shows a fair picture of the world, or that a photo they took is theirs alone to post. Another slip is thinking that deleting a post removes it. Others may already have copied it.","In the lab your child likes posts and watches six feed slots narrow. Then they search for something new to widen it. They raise a post's shock level and see its reach over four rounds grow from 5 people to 781. They pick which checks of a scary health claim help. They tap four tricks in a viral post, and decide to post, ask first or not post in six cases."]},"familyTip":"Pick a viral post together this week. Ask: who made this, and what do other sites say about them? Open a new tab and check. Then swap feeds for five minutes and compare: what does each of your apps think you like?","keyIdea":"Apps learn from what you like and show you more of it, so your feed can shrink into a filter bubble. False posts often spread further and faster than true ones, because shock, anger and surprise make people share before they check. Anyone can be fooled, so use habits, not gut feeling: pause, read sideways by opening new tabs to see what others say about a source, and look for tricks like rush, fear and one-person stories. Be the same person online as off, and ask before posting anyone else's photo or details.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each habit a real check or a false comfort? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Real check","False comfort"],"items":[["Search what other sites say about the source",0],["It has thousands of likes",1],["Look for the original study or report",0],["It feels true to me",1],["Check a fact-checking site",0],["The site's About page says it is trusted",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfeed","caption":"Flagship lab: watch a feed narrow into a filter bubble, see shocking posts spread, read sideways, spot tricks and ask before posting.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_RhntJz7p_6":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Your friend takes your pencil without asking. What is the best first step?","choices":["Stop and breathe","Grab it back","Hide their pencil"],"answer":0,"hint":"The first step comes before you do anything at all.","explain":"Stop and breathe first. Then you can think of options, like asking for it back."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why should you think of more than one option?","choices":["So you can compare them and pick the best","So it takes longer","You should not, the first is best"],"answer":0,"hint":"With only one option, can you really choose?","explain":"With two or more options you can compare what each would do and pick the best one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Eating all your sweets now feels great. What might happen later?","choices":["A tummy ache, and none left","Nothing at all","More sweets appear"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about tomorrow, not just right now.","explain":"A choice can feel good now and bad later. Eating them all could mean a tummy ache and no sweets tomorrow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"When you think about consequences, whose feelings count?","choices":["Yours and other people's","Only yours","Nobody's"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who else might a choice affect?","explain":"A choice can affect other people as well as you, so think about everyone it touches."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You think of {n} options. 2 of them could hurt someone. How many options are left to choose from?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take away the options that could hurt someone.","explain":"{n} take away 2 is {=n-2} options left. Thinking about consequences helps you drop the bad ones.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Your choice did not work. What should you do?","choices":["Go back and try another option","Give up","Do the same thing again, louder"],"answer":0,"hint":"You thought of more than one option. Where are the others?","explain":"If one option fails, go back to your list and try the next one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} children want the one swing. Which option is most likely to work for everyone?","choices":["Take turns, with a timer","The fastest runner gets it","Everyone pushes to get on"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which option gives all {n} children a go and keeps everyone safe?","explain":"Taking turns gives all {n} children a go. Racing or pushing leaves some out or gets someone hurt.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which one is a long-term consequence?","choices":["Getting better at reading by next year","Feeling happy this minute","Laughing at a joke right now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Long-term means a long time after the choice.","explain":"Getting better by next year happens a long time later, so it is long-term. The others happen right now."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A friend cheated and asks you to keep it a secret. Which option gives them a chance to own up?","choices":["Talk to your friend first","Tell the whole class","Pretend you saw nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which option lets your friend fix it themselves?","explain":"Talking first gives your friend a chance to own up. Telling everyone shames them, and pretending lets the cheating go on."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the first step when something goes wrong?","choices":["Stop and take a breath","Do your first idea fast","Shout so people listen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Before you think of options, what gives you time?","explain":"Stopping first gives you time to think. A first idea comes fast and is often not the best one."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You think of {n} options for a problem. Then you think of 1 more. How many options do you have now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count on 1 from the options you had.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1} options. More options give you more to compare.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is a consequence?","choices":["What happens because of a choice","The first idea you have","A rule at school"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think: I chose this, so then that happened.","explain":"A consequence is what happens because of a choice. It can be good or bad, now or later."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You have {n} sweets. You eat 2 now and save the rest for later. How many do you save?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take the 2 you eat away from all your sweets.","explain":"{n} take away 2 is {=n-2}, so you save {=n-2} sweets. Thinking about later means some are still left tomorrow.","params":{"n":[6,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which reason shows you thought about what will happen?","choices":["I will tidy up first, so I can find my toys later","It was my first idea","I just felt like it"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good reason says what will happen because of the choice.","explain":"Finding your toys later is a consequence, so that reason shows you thought ahead. The others do not say what will happen."}],"explain":{"kid":["When something goes wrong, a first idea pops up fast. Often it is to shout, grab or hit back. Stop! Take a breath before you do anything. Stopping gives you time to think.","Next, think of your options. An option is one thing you could do. There is nearly always more than one: say how you feel, fix it together, or ask a grown-up for help.","Then think about what might happen with each option. That is its consequence. Think about now and later, and about you and other people. A choice can feel great now and bad tomorrow.","Now choose, and know why. A good reason says what will happen: I will do my homework first, so it gets done and I still get to play. If your choice does not work, go back and try your next option."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. This matches responsible decision-making in the CASEL framework: name the problem, think of ways to solve it, and weigh what is likely to happen for you and for others. It also matches the IB PYP personal, social and physical education goal of using a simple process when making a choice. Key words: decision, choice, option, consequence, short-term, long-term, impulse, reason, review.","Stopping before acting uses inhibitory control. This is one of the core executive functions, and it is still growing through childhood (Diamond, 2013). Programmes such as Stop Think Do teach a short routine. Stop and calm down. Think of options and what each would lead to. Then do the one you chose, and check how it went. Practise it on small, everyday problems, so it is easier to use when feelings run high.","Common mistakes: acting on the first idea; thinking there is only one option; thinking only about now, not later; thinking only about yourself; giving a reason that is not about what will happen, such as it was my first idea; and giving up, or doing the same thing again, when a choice does not work.","In the lab your child stops a first idea before it happens and finds three more options. They match four options to what is likely to happen when a friend cheats. They slide from now to next week to compare two choices. They pick a choice and a reason, and find two good next tries when a choice fails."]},"familyTip":"When a small problem comes up at home, like two people wanting the same thing, say the steps out loud together: Stop. What are our options? What might happen with each one? Then choose, and later ask: did it work?","keyIdea":"When you face a choice, stop before you act on your first idea. Think of more than one option, and think about what might happen with each one: now and later, for you and for other people. Then choose, and be able to say why. If your choice does not work, go back and try another option.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"First idea or thought-through choice? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Acting on a first idea","Stopping to think"],"items":[["Grab the ball back",0],["Count to five, then say how I feel",1],["Shout the answer out",0],["Ask what else I could do",1],["Hit back",0],["Think what might happen next",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstc","caption":"Flagship lab: stop a first idea, find more options, match options to consequences, compare now and later, choose with a reason.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_JivEBTD_KV":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is peer pressure?","choices":["A push from people your age to act like them","A rule set by your teacher","Your own idea to try something"],"answer":0,"hint":"Peers are people your own age.","explain":"Peer pressure comes from people your own age, and can be helpful or harmful."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"Everyone in our year has done it.\" Why is this a trick?","choices":["It is often not true, and it is still your choice","It is always true","It means it must be safe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do people often guess more of their friends do risky things than really do?","explain":"People often overestimate how many peers do risky things, and even if many did, you still decide."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In the line study, what helped people most to give their own answer?","choices":["One other person agreeing with them","Being in a bigger group","Waiting to answer last"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the round where one person said B.","explain":"With one ally, going along with the wrong group fell from about 1 in 3 answers to about 1 in 20."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A friend tells you someone is hurting them and says, \"Don't tell anyone.\" What should you do?","choices":["Tell a trusted adult","Keep the secret","Post about it online"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this a secret that keeps someone safe, or one that keeps them in danger?","explain":"When someone is being hurt, tell a trusted adult. That is not breaking a friendship."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You practise {n} ways to say no, and use {k} of them in a role-play. How many have you still to try?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the ones you used away from the ones you practised.","explain":"{n} ways take away {k} used leaves {=n-k} still to try.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"\"If you were my real friend, you would.\" Which reply works best?","choices":["\"I am your friend. I'm still not doing it.\"","\"OK, I'll prove it.\"","\"Then I hate you.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"You can keep the friendship and the no at the same time.","explain":"A real friend accepts your no. You can say you are friends and still refuse."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} kids are teased into a dare. Each one has a friend who says no with them, making pairs. How many people are saying no?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each kid plus their ally makes two people.","explain":"{n} pairs of 2 make {=2*n} people saying no together.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Your friends all wear the same trainers, so you want some too. Is this OK?","choices":["Usually fine: nobody is hurt and you still choose","Never OK: all peer influence is bad","Only OK if you lie about the price"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: could someone be hurt? Would I feel bad later?","explain":"Copying friends in harmless ways is normal. The line is when someone could be hurt."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why does walking away count as resisting?","choices":["You leave the pressure and keep your choice","It shows you agree","It means you lost"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do you have to win an argument to say no?","explain":"Walking away ends the push without a fight, and you still did not give in."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a helpful push from friends?","choices":["Getting you to join the choir with them","Daring you to climb the school fence","Telling you to leave a new kid out"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one leads to something good for you and harms nobody?","explain":"Joining the choir is good for you and hurts nobody. That is positive peer pressure."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"This week Leo felt {n} pushes from friends. {k} were harmful and he said no to each. How many pushes were helpful?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Every push was either helpful or harmful. Take away the harmful ones.","explain":"{n} pushes take away {k} harmful ones leaves {=n-k} helpful pushes.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Go on, are you scared?\" Which trick is this?","choices":["A dare or tease","\"Just this once\"","A helpful push"],"answer":0,"hint":"It calls you a name so you feel you must prove it wrong.","explain":"Calling you scared is a dare or tease. It makes saying no feel like losing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A group of {n} friends tries to get you to skip class. One of them, Asha, says no with you. How many are still pushing?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Asha is now on your side. Take her away from the group.","explain":"{n} friends minus Asha leaves {=n-1} still pushing, and now you have an ally.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which reply to a dare is calm and firm?","choices":["\"No thanks. I'm going to play football.\"","\"Fine, but only once.\"","\"You're all so stupid.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a clear no that does not insult anyone.","explain":"A short no with a new plan is calm and firm. Giving in or insulting makes it worse."}],"explain":{"kid":["Peer pressure is the push you feel from people your age to act like them. Sometimes they say it out loud. Sometimes you just feel it, because you want to fit in.","Not all of it is bad. A friend who gets you to join a club or try the climbing wall is a helpful push. A harmful push gets you to do something unkind, risky or against the rules.","Harmful pushes use tricks. A dare calls you scared. Everyone does it makes you feel odd for saying no, and it is often not even true. Just this once makes it sound small. Real friends would uses your friendship as a prize. Once you can name the trick, it is easier to say no.","Groups are strong. In one study, people saw a group give a clearly wrong answer, and about 3 in 4 went along at least once. But with just one person on their side, most stuck with the truth. So be that one person for a friend. To say no, keep it calm and short, use a joke and a new plan, or walk away. If someone could get hurt, tell a trusted adult."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 11. The PSHE Association programme for Key Stage 2 asks pupils to spot and manage dares and pressure from friends. They should also know ways to respond, and when and how to ask for help. Key terms: peer pressure, dare, social norm, assertive, trusted adult.","Pressure from peers can be spoken, like a dare, or unspoken, like the wish to fit in. It can be good as well as bad. Social norms research by Perkins and Berkowitz found that young people often think more of their peers take risks than really do. That is why 'everyone does it' works so well. In Asch's line studies in the 1950s, people went along with a wrong group on about a third of key trials. About 3 in 4 went along at least once. With one honest partner, that fell to about 1 in 20.","Common mistakes: thinking all peer pressure is bad, so children miss the good kind and cannot tell the two apart; thinking that saying no means a big fight, when insults tend to make the push stronger; accepting 'just once' as if it does not count; staying quiet when a friend is pushed, though one ally is the best defence; and keeping a secret when someone is being hurt. Be careful not to treat giving in as a flaw. Adults go along with groups too. The skill is to spot the trick and have a plan.","In the lab your child sorts six pushes as helpful or harmful and names the trick in four messages. Then they judge line lengths while five classmates give a wrong answer, and again with one ally. They pick replies to three rising pushes, and decide if eight choices are fine to copy or need a no or a trusted adult. Each wrong choice gets a reason that names the slip."]},"familyTip":"Swap stories at dinner: each person tells a time a friend pushed them, once in a good way and once in a bad way, and what they did. Then practise three ways to say no to a dare, out loud, in a calm voice: a plain no, a joke with a new plan, and simply walking off. Agree a code word your child can text you if they need an easy way out of a situation.","keyIdea":"Peer pressure is the push you feel from people your own age to think or act like them. It can help you, like friends getting you to try a new club, or harm you, like a dare to take something or share a mean photo. Harmful pushes use a few tricks: dares and teasing, \"everyone does it\", \"just this once\" and \"real friends would\". In a famous study, about 3 in 4 people went along with a group's clearly wrong answer at least once, but one person on their side made going along far rarer. You can resist with a calm no, a joke and a new plan, or by walking away, and you tell a trusted adult when someone could be hurt.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which reply resists the pressure well? Drag each reply, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Resists well","Gives in or starts a fight"],"items":[["\"No thanks, I'm good.\"",0],["\"OK, just this once.\"",1],["\"Ha, not today. Who wants to play tag?\"",0],["\"You're all idiots.\"",1],["Walking away to find another friend",0],["Laughing along so they stop looking at you",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpeer","caption":"Flagship lab: sort helpful from harmful pushes, name the trick, trust your eyes against a crowd, hold your line, decide where the line is.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_GC5qGmVTiC":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You fall over and hurt your knee at the park. What do you do?","choices":["Tell a grown-up I know","Hide it and say nothing","Wait and hope it goes away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who can help make it better?","explain":"Tell a grown-up you know. You will not be in trouble, and they can clean your knee and help."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Auntie wants a kiss. You do not want one. What can you do?","choices":["Say no thank you and wave","Kiss her anyway","Run off and hide"],"answer":0,"hint":"Your body belongs to you.","explain":"You can say no thank you and give a wave or a high five. Auntie can still know you love her."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You tell your teacher you are sad, but she is busy and does not hear. What next?","choices":["Tell her again, or tell another grown-up","Stop telling anyone","Shout at her"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do not give up. Who else is on your hand?","explain":"Keep telling. Ask her again when she is free, or tell another grown-up on your hand."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam has {n} trusted grown-ups on his helping hand. A man he does not know asks him to come and see some puppies. What should Sam do?","choices":["Say no and go to his grown-up","Go with the man","Keep it a secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Sam's grown-up say yes?","explain":"Sam should say no and go to his grown-up straight away, then tell them. Only go with someone your grown-up said yes to.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which one is a time to tell a grown-up?","choices":["A big child keeps pushing me","I am playing with my friend","I got a sticker"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one feels bad or unsafe?","explain":"Being pushed again and again is a time to tell. A grown-up can stop it. The others are happy moments."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You are lost in a shop. A shop worker asks your name. What do you say?","choices":["My name and my grown-up's name","Nothing at all","I will find them myself"],"answer":0,"hint":"What helps the shop worker find your grown-up?","explain":"Say your name and your grown-up's name. Then the shop worker can call for them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"It is {n} days to Dad's birthday. You and Mum have a present for him. Is it OK to keep it secret?","choices":["Yes, it is a happy surprise","No, tell Dad now","Yes, keep it for ever"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it a happy surprise or a worry?","explain":"It is a happy surprise, so keep it until his birthday. Then everyone knows. A secret that makes you feel bad should be told.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why do we tell a grown-up when something feels wrong?","choices":["So they can help keep us safe","So someone gets told off","Because we are naughty"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do trusted grown-ups do?","explain":"Telling helps a grown-up keep you safe. It is never naughty to tell when you feel hurt, worried or scared."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is a trusted grown-up?","choices":["My teacher","A person I do not know who smiles","Anyone with sweets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Trusted grown-ups are people you know well.","explain":"Your teacher knows you and keeps you safe. A smile or sweets does not make a person someone you can trust."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ben is in a big shop with {n} aisles. He cannot see his dad. What should he do?","choices":["Stay in the shop and ask a shop worker","Go outside to look","Hide behind a shelf"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where will Dad look for Ben?","explain":"Ben should stay inside and ask a shop worker at the till. Dad will look for him in the shop. If he hides or goes outside, he is harder to find.","params":{"n":[4,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ava's cousin keeps tickling her. She has said stop {n} times. What can she do now?","choices":["Tell a grown-up","Let it go on","Bite her cousin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ava said stop. It did not stop. Who can help?","explain":"Ava should tell a grown-up. Her body belongs to her, and a grown-up can make the tickling stop.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which secret should you tell a grown-up about?","choices":["Someone hurt me and said do not tell","A present for Mum's birthday","A party for Dad on Sunday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the secret make you feel happy or bad?","explain":"A secret about being hurt is a worry secret, so tell it. A present or a party is a happy surprise that everyone finds out about soon."}],"explain":{"kid":["A trusted grown-up is someone you know well who keeps you safe. Mum, Dad, Gran, your teacher or your nursery helper can be one. A smile or sweets does not make a person safe.","Tell a trusted grown-up when you feel hurt, sad, scared or worried. Tell them when someone is unkind to you. You will not be in trouble for telling. They want to help.","Lost in a shop? Stop. Stay inside. Ask a shop worker at the till, or a mum or dad with children. Say your name.","Your body belongs to you. You can say no to a kiss, a hug or a tickle you do not want. A surprise is OK to keep for now. A worry secret is not. Tell it. If a grown-up is busy, tell another one."],"adult":["Ages 4 to 5, read it together. In the EYFS, Personal, Social and Emotional Development asks children to manage their own needs and to ask for help. The DfE's Relationships Education guidance (\"Being safe\") goes further. Children learn that each person's body belongs to them. They learn that some secrets should not be kept, and to keep asking for help until they are heard. The NSPCC's PANTS rules give the same messages at this age.","The lab builds a small safety plan. Your child names five trusted adults, one per finger. They learn which moments to tell: hurt, worried, scared, lost, or someone being unkind. If lost in a shop, they stay inside, go to a shop worker or a parent with children, and say their name. They practise saying no to touch they do not want. They sort happy surprises from worry secrets. Last, they learn to tell someone else if the first adult is busy.","Common mistakes: children often think telling is 'telling tales', or that they will be in trouble. When lost, they may run outside or hide. They may think anyone friendly is safe. They may keep a secret because a grown-up asked them to. Many give up after one busy adult. Adults slip too: making a child hug a relative, or brushing off a small worry, teaches that telling does not work. Thank your child every time they tell you something."]},"familyTip":"Trace your child's hand on paper and write one trusted grown-up on each finger together. Stick it on the fridge. In a shop, point out who wears a staff badge or works at the till, and practise saying 'I'm lost. My name is…' together.","keyIdea":"Trusted grown-ups are people you know well who keep you safe, like a parent, a grandparent, your teacher or a nursery helper. A smile or sweets does not make someone a trusted grown-up. When you feel hurt, worried, scared, lost or uncomfortable, tell one of them. If you get lost in a shop, stay inside and ask a shop worker or a mum or dad with children. Your body belongs to you, so you can say no to a kiss, hug or tickle you do not want. A surprise is fine to keep for a little while, but a worry secret should be told. If the first grown-up is busy, keep telling until someone listens.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Should you tell a grown-up, or is it all OK? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Tell a grown-up","All OK"],"items":[["I hurt my finger",0],["I am building a tower",1],["Someone keeps hitting me",0],["I am lost",0],["I am singing a song",1],["A secret makes me feel bad",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtrust","caption":"Flagship lab: fill your helping hand, sort tell-or-OK moments, get help when lost in a shop, say no, sort secrets and keep telling.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_AT0HOVXLlP":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Dev is 20. What is the lowest legal hourly rate for him from April 2026?","choices":["£10.85","£12.71","£8.00"],"answer":0,"hint":"18 to 20 has its own rate.","explain":"From 1 April 2026 the 18 to 20 rate is £10.85. £12.71 starts at 21."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Lucy is 18 and works 7 hours. What break is she owed?","choices":["20 minutes","30 minutes","None if the shop is busy"],"answer":0,"hint":"At 18 she is an adult worker.","explain":"Adult workers get 20 minutes when the day is over 6 hours. The 30-minute rule is for under 18s."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Tom is 24 and works {h} hours at 1271 pence an hour (£12.71). What is his gross pay in pence?","answer":"h*1271","hint":"Multiply the hours by 1271 pence.","explain":"{h} × 1271 = {=h*1271} pence before any tax or deductions.","params":{"h":[5,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Who has the right to an itemised payslip?","choices":["Every employee and worker, every payday","Only full-time staff","Only people who ask"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it a legal right or a favour?","explain":"All employees and workers have the right to an itemised payslip on or before payday."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which of these is your responsibility at work?","choices":["Following the safety training you were given","Writing your own payslip","Setting the minimum wage"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does your employer expect from you?","explain":"You must follow safety training and work reliably. Payslips are the employer's job; the government sets the minimum wage."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Your pay is still wrong after you talked to your manager. What is a good next step?","choices":["Raise a formal grievance in writing","Stop going in without telling anyone","Post about it online first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put the problem on record inside work first.","explain":"A written grievance starts the employer's formal process. Acas can give free advice at any stage."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Kit is 18 and paid 17360 pence for 16 hours, but a {d} pence uniform charge is taken off. What is his pay after the deduction, in pence?","answer":"17360-d","hint":"Take {d} away from 17360, then compare with 16 × 1085 = 17360.","explain":"17360 − {d} = {=17360-d} pence. That is below the 17360 pence minimum for 16 hours, so the deduction is not allowed.","params":{"d":[1000,4000]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Grace is 22. In May 2026 her payslip shows £12.21 an hour. What is wrong?","choices":["£12.21 was the old rate; she needs at least £12.71","Nothing, it is above £10.85","She should get £13.21"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rates change on 1 April each year.","explain":"£12.21 was the 21+ rate until 31 March 2026. From 1 April 2026 it is £12.71."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Jo is 17. What is the lowest legal hourly rate for her from 1 April 2026?","choices":["£8.00","£12.71","There is no minimum under 18"],"answer":0,"hint":"Under 18 has its own rate. It is the same as the apprentice rate.","explain":"From 1 April 2026 the minimum for 16 and 17 year olds is £8.00 an hour."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Sam is 19 and works {h} hours at the 18 to 20 minimum, 1085 pence an hour (£10.85). What is his gross pay in pence?","answer":"h*1085","hint":"Gross pay in pence is hours × 1085: {h} × 1085.","explain":"{h} × 1085 = {=h*1085} pence before any tax or deductions. 100 pence make £1.","params":{"h":[4,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Priya is 22 and is paid 1250 pence an hour for {h} hours. The minimum is 1271 pence. How many pence short is she?","answer":"h*21","hint":"She is 21 pence short for every hour. Multiply 21 by {h}.","explain":"1271 − 1250 = 21 pence an hour. × {h} hours = {=h*21} pence short.","params":{"h":[10,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ana is 17 and works a 5-hour shift. What break is she owed by law?","choices":["30 minutes","None: only after 6 hours","20 minutes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Under 18 the limit is 4½ hours, not 6.","explain":"Young workers under 18 get a 30-minute break when they work more than 4½ hours. 5 hours is more."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Kit's boss takes £30 for his uniform, which drops his pay below the minimum wage. Is that allowed?","choices":["No","Yes, the uniform is his cost","Yes, if he signed for it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out his pay per hour after the deduction.","explain":"A deduction for a uniform you must wear counts against minimum wage pay, so it cannot take you below the minimum, even if you signed."}],"explain":{"kid":["When you start a job in the UK, the law sets the lowest hourly pay you can get. It depends on your age. From 1 April 2026 it is £12.71 an hour at 21 and over, £10.85 at 18 to 20, and £8.00 if you are under 18 or a new apprentice. The rates go up each April.","Your employer must give you a payslip every payday. Check three things. Do the hours match your rota? Is the rate at least the minimum for your age? Does any deduction, such as a charge for your uniform, take you below the minimum? If it does, that is not allowed.","You also get rest breaks. At 18 or over, a shift of more than 6 hours gives you a 20-minute break. Under 18, more than 4½ hours gives you 30 minutes. You get paid holiday and a written statement of your job terms from your first day.","Rights come with responsibilities. Turn up on time, follow the safety training and tell your manager early if you are ill. If your pay or breaks are wrong, check your payslip, then talk to your manager. If that fails, write a formal complaint called a grievance. Acas gives free advice, and HMRC can make an employer pay what you are owed."],"adult":["Ages 16 to 18. The PSHE Association programme of study for key stage 5 covers rights and responsibilities at work, including pay, contracts and where to get help. The rates here come from the National Minimum Wage (Amendment) Regulations 2026, in force from 1 April 2026: £12.71 (21+), £10.85 (18 to 20), £8.00 (16 to 17) and £8.00 (apprentices under 19 or in the first year).","Under the Working Time Regulations 1998, adult workers get a 20-minute uninterrupted break when the working day is longer than 6 hours. Young workers above school-leaving age but under 18 get 30 minutes when it is longer than 4.5 hours. Breaks need not be paid. Statutory holiday is 5.6 weeks a year, pro rata for part-time. Employees and workers have the right to an itemised payslip and a written statement of particulars from day one. Deductions for items such as a required uniform count against minimum wage pay.","Common mistakes: thinking under-18s have no minimum wage; using last year's rate after 1 April; thinking a deduction is fine because the worker signed for it. Others apply the under-18 break rule to an 18-year-old, or think a break is owed on any adult shift. Some treat a payslip as a favour. Some walk out or post online before they raise a written grievance. In the lab each slip gets feedback that names it."]},"familyTip":"Look at a real or sample payslip together. Find the hours, the hourly rate, the gross pay and each deduction, and check the rate against the gov.uk minimum wage page for the worker's age.","keyIdea":"In the UK the law sets a minimum hourly rate that depends on your age. From 1 April 2026 it is £12.71 at 21 and over (the National Living Wage), £10.85 at 18 to 20, and £8.00 under 18 or as an apprentice under 19 or in the first year. Every worker has the right to an itemised payslip, a written statement of their main terms from day one, paid holiday and rest breaks: 20 minutes for a shift over 6 hours at 18 and over, or 30 minutes over 4½ hours if under 18. Deductions such as a uniform charge cannot take your pay below the minimum. Your side is to be reliable, on time and to follow safety training. If something is wrong, check your payslip and contract, talk to your manager, raise a written grievance, then get free advice from Acas or report unpaid minimum wage to HMRC.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is this pay legal from 1 April 2026? Drag each job to a box, or tap the job and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Legal","Below the minimum"],"items":[["Age 18, £10.85 an hour",0],["Age 25, £12.71 an hour",0],["Age 16, £8.00 an hour",0],["Age 21, £11.50 an hour",1],["Age 17, £7.00 an hour",1],["Age 19, £10.00 an hour",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagworkright","caption":"Flagship lab: sort pay offers by age, find the error on three payslips, set legal rest breaks, sort rights from duties and plan a fix.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
